ofactorless curve point validation.\n\nThe specification requires strict verification to be consistent across all implementations. This means the implementation must follow a single, unambiguous verification path rather than differing between cofactored and cofactorless curve point checks.\n\nThe contract door rule 3.4 requires verifying the signature with the party's key over the contract signing bytes. A false or unusable result rejects the contract. The public key test of section 3.2 applies to party keys during signature verification.\n\nI have settled on cofactorless Ed25519 verification. When the public key and signature point both pass the prime-order subgroup test, cofactored and cofactorless checks agree. I will decode points from 32-byte little-endian encoding by extracting y from the first 255 bits, reading the sign bit from the last byte, recovering x from the curve equation, and rejecting any y that is not less than p.\n\nI am defining the strict point validation for Ed25519 signatures. A decoded point is rejected if it does not satisfy the curve equation or if its canonical encoding differs from the re-encoded bytes. The prime-order subgroup test will multiply the point by L and verify it equals the identity, while rejecting small-order and mixed-order points.\n\nI am now examining canonical JSON requirements. Python's json.dumps handles NaN, Infinity, and finite floats in a way that must be replicated exactly to avoid non-canonical output.\n\nI have identified the exact canonical JSON float formatting rules. Python's json.dumps outputs NaN, Infinity, and -Infinity as uppercase strings. Finite floats use repr-style formatting such as 1.5, 1e+16, or 1.0. Integers must be written without leading zeros.\n\nI will implement custom canonical JSON serialization rather than using json.dumps. Floats will be formatted to match Python's repr output exactly.\n\nI have identified the exact Python repr() outputs for canonical JSON floats: 1e+16, 1.0, and 1e-07.\n\nI will build a custom JSON parser instead of using the standard library. It must reject duplicate keys, enforce a depth limit of 512, track numbers as integer or float tokens, treat NaN, Infinity, and -Infinity as valid non-integer values, reject invalid variants like nan or +Infinity, treat 1e400 as infinity, reject integers longer than 4300 digits, and only accept true integer tokens.\n\nThe parser will preserve number kinds so that mathematically integer floats like 1.0 remain as non-integer tokens and are written back as 1.0. Depth is measured by bracket nesting levels, not by parsed value depth; a document deeper than 512 levels of brackets is an error.\n\nI have clarified the depth counting rule: each `{` or `[` outside strings increments the nesting level, and exceeding 512 is an error.\n\nThe computation flow from section 2.3 proceeds as follows: compute the contract SHA-256 hash, bind records by it, triage every bound record, check contract terms, verify header view, and collect all problems found without stopping other checks.\n\nThe remaining computation flow is now clear. If any problem is found in rules 4.1, 4.2, or 5.1 rules 1 through 8, no record is examined further. Otherwise the program collapses copies, checks conformance and anchors, and records that pass form the event set. That event set is then walked, checked against NENRIN, and judged by the final verdict. Several later checks are collected but never stop the computation.\n\nA failing record under rule 6.0 is excluded from the event set but does not halt processing of other records. The same applies to rule 7.4: a record below the lower bound receives disposition anchor_below_lower_bound and is not accepted, yet the remaining computation continues. The specification explicitly states that reasons from rules 4.3, 5.1 rule 9, 6.0, 7.4, and 11 never empty the event set.\n\nI am determining which reasons empty the event set. Rules 4.1, 4.2, and 5.1 rules 1 through 8 halt processing entirely, leaving the event set empty. Rule 6.2 also empties it when more than 10,000 records are kept. I still need to verify whether rules 7.4, 7.5, and 7.6 stop the walk or merely mark records as underspecified.\n\nAny single record failing 7.4, 7.5, or 7.6 makes the entire settlement underspecified. The first failure decides the disposition for that record alone, so other records continue processing. Section 12 confirms that a single underspecified reason empties the deviations.\n\nI am checking whether anchor failures from 7.4–7.6, such as anchor_proof_invalid or anchor_batch_leg_refused, empty the event set or merely mark records as underspecified. The specification only explicitly states that 7.4 below the lower bound does not empty it, so I need to determine if the walk still runs when other anchor checks fail.\n\nThe walk proceeds even when anchor failures add underspecified reasons. Section 12 empties deviations only if any underspecified reason exists, but the walk itself still runs.\n\nThe walk proceeds against the grant and NENRIN checks are applied. Evidence missing is only added when the verdict would be within_grant or deviation; it is skipped if the settlement is already underspecified. The final verdict is determined by collecting all underspecified reasons and deviations first.\n\nThe final verdict is decided by a strict priority chain: underspecified first, then deviation, then nothing_to_settle if the event set is empty, and finally within_grant. The evidence_missing check only applies when the verdict would be within_grant or deviation; adding it to an already-deviation verdict leaves it as deviation. The settlement status is undetermined if underspecified, final if all accepted records are accepted and orphaned heights do not exceed the horizon, and provisional otherwise.\n\nBond outcomes depend on the final verdict and status. Dispositions are assigned to every classified record. Records rejected by 4.1, 4.2, or 5.1 rules 1 through 8 receive no disposition because they are never examined further. Binding still classifies them as foreign, inconsistent, or unbound. Rule 6.0 triage occurs before the contract terms and header view checks.\n\nI am placing the 6.2 size limit after triage and before collapse, conformance, and anchor checks. A header view exceeding 200,000 headers also halts examination entirely, but I still need to determine whether it adds a chain_view_rejected reason.\n\nThe 200,000-header limit adds only input_too_large and does not also add chain_view_rejected. Rule 9 view_not_strict is separate and does not stop the walk. Out-of-scope keys such as spine or approvers must not be processed; an implementation claiming core v0 conformance on them is invalid. The program should treat out-of-scope fields as non-2.2 errors.\n\nOut-of-scope contracts will trigger an error exit rather than a fake core v0 result.\n\nI am refining the out-of-scope boundary. A null limits or admission field is not carried, so those are not out-of-scope; approvers that fail well-formedness are rejected under 4.1 instead. Only requirements.spine, requirements.convergence, and a well-formed grant.approval_policy.approvers list count as out of scope.\n\nA key is carried only when present with a non-null value. Null limits or admission are therefore not carried, so they do not trigger out-of-scope rejection. A well-formed approvers list is carried and out of scope; any other present approvers field is refused under 4.1 as contract_not_verified.\n\nI am reconsidering the out-of-scope boundary. The test vector synth_parent_approvers_child_drops indicates a child carrying approvers is out of scope, while a parent with approvers is tested under core v0. I need to determine whether 4.1 rule 10 makes a parent with well-formed approvers out of scope.\n\nThe out-of-scope boundary applies to the contract being settled, not its parent. A parent with pinned approvers is valid under core v0; only a child carrying those keys is out of scope.\n\nI will implement a check that returns an \"out_of_scope\" error when the contract carries out-of-scope keys. I am now re-reading section 4.1 carefully to implement all its rules.\n\nI also clarified the interaction between 4.1 and 4.3. When a parent is supplied, 4.3.1 judges whether the parent itself passes 4.1 and adds only the reason parent_narrowing_failed. The final determination of contract_not_verified is still made by 4.1 on the contract alone.\n\nI am examining whether a contract that fails 4.1 on its own also triggers parent_narrowing_failed under 4.3.1, since the specification states that passing with a parent requires passing on its own.\n\nI am checking whether a contract that fails 4.1 on its own also receives parent_narrowing_failed under 4.3, or if the two reasons remain independent.\n\nThe 4.3 table indicates that a contract failing 4.1 on its own also triggers parent_narrowing_failed, since it does not pass 4.1 with the parent supplied. This conflicts with revision 0.6, which states that failing 4.1 with the parent supplied adds only parent_narrowing_failed and never contract_not_verified.\n\nI will apply both contract_not_verified and parent_narrowing_failed when a contract fails 4.1 on its own while a parent is supplied. The standalone 4.1 check decides the former, and the parent-door check decides the latter. If the contract fails 4.1, 4.2 is skipped but 4.3 still runs.\n\nThe `contract_signature_not_strict` reason is added whenever a signature decodes to 64 bytes but is not strict, regardless of whether 4.1 accepts the contract. A signature that fails decoding or verification does not trigger this reason.\n\nI am now detailing the 6.0 triage logic. Records whose SHA-256 cannot be computed are set aside with reason `record_not_strict` and receive no disposition. The program does not output the `informative v1_16.refused` count. I am also considering when `record_sha256` might fail to compute, such as with non-finite values, though canonicalization may handle them.\n\nTriage applies only to records that binding successfully digests. The first triage step sets aside any record whose signature is not strictly signed or whose body is not strictly canonical, assigning it the reason `record_not_strict` and no disposition. Records with SHA-256 computation failures are also set aside under this same reason.\n\nLax signatures that verify under 3.4 alone are set aside with reason `signature_not_strict`. Strictly signed records with non-strict bodies are set aside with `record_not_strict` and the underspecified reason `record_not_strict`. If a strictly signed record has a non-strict anchor but another bound record shares its signing bytes and anchors validly, it is set aside with `anchor_not_strict`; otherwise it is underspecified as `anchor_not_strict`.\n\nRow 7 sets aside revocation records when grant.revocation is not an object, using reason revocation_not_provided_for. Row 8 keeps all remaining records. Row 6 assigns disposition and reason anchor_not_strict, then sets the record aside. Row 5 sets aside a record with disposition anchor_not_strict but no reason, because another bound record anchors it validly.\n\nI am resolving a contradiction in the triage rules. The first three rows set aside or reject records without adding a reason. The fourth row sets aside a record and adds the reason record_not_strict. Row 5 sets aside a record with disposition anchor_not_strict but no reason. Row 6 sets aside a record and adds the reason anchor_not_strict. I am checking whether row 6 also carries a disposition, since section 9.2 says anchor_not_strict is a disposition for set aside or failing closed.\n\nRow 6 assigns disposition `anchor_not_strict` and reason `anchor_not_strict`, setting the record aside. Row 5 assigns disposition `anchor_not_strict` but no reason, also setting the record aside. The program must verify that another bound record with identical signing bytes anchors validly before applying row 5.\n\nTriage runs before section 5 validation, so the \"anchors validly\" check in row 5 is circular. If the view fails section 5, no record anchors validly, so row 5 does not apply and row 6 underspecifies as anchor_not_strict.\n\nI am defining the candidate kept record for row 5. A bound record qualifies only if it is strictly signed, its body is strictly canonical, its anchor is strictly canonical, and it is either not a revocation or carries a valid grant.revocation object.\n\nA strictly signed record with a non-strict anchor is set aside without reason only if another bound record with identical signing bytes is kept and anchors validly. If that other record is a revocation lacking a policy, it is not kept, so the non-strict anchor copy is not saved. A non-strict anchor record is also not kept.\n\n\"Anchors validly\" requires the view to hold the height, a matching block hash, a proof reaching the Merkle root, and a batch leg that accepts. It does not compare against the checkpoint. If the view fails section 5 rules 1-8, the program treats the record as not anchoring validly.\n\nThe other bound record must differ by SHA-256 but share signing bytes. If two copies both carry non-strict anchors, the table sets both aside without reason. A strict-anchor copy that anchors validly is kept by the table, yet it still requires a valid anchor to qualify for row 5; otherwise row 6 applies. The phrase \"kept by this table\" means passing triage, not passing section 7.\n\nA kept record that does not anchor validly causes the non-strict anchor copy to fail closed under row 6. The program should only evaluate whether a record anchors validly after the view passes section 5 rules 1-8, because an invalid view yields meaningless heights and hashes. If the grant is missing or not an object, revocations are set aside by row 7 even when 4.1 fails, since triage runs before the stop.\n\nIf the contract is not a JSON object, the settlement is underspecified with reason `contract_not_verified` and no further computation occurs. If events is not a list, it is underspecified with reason `input_not_settleable`. In both cases, `contract_sha256` is null.\n\nThe file interface guarantees that events is always a list, so the input_not_settleable disposition cannot arise from the standard input format. A file in the events directory that fails to parse is treated as a 2.2 error and stops the settlement. Duplicate keys anywhere in the input files are also treated as errors.\n\n6.6 conformance applies only to kept records after 6.4, provided the settlement did not stop early. A record with a conformance problem is rejected unless its contractor signature verifies under 3.4, in which case it becomes a charge with deviation nonconforming_record and proceeds to section 7. Witness-signed executions with conformance problems are rejected because they lack a contractor signature.\n\nThe conformance check does not read other records, so rejecting them in a single pass is sufficient. The specification's note that no check reads a rejected record means later steps can simply exclude rejected records from their inputs.\n\n6.4 collapse runs before 6.6 conformance. Rejected records are never examined again, so the collapse step only sees records kept by triage. Records are grouped by signing bytes; if multiple distinct SHA-256 hashes exist, the one with the smallest height and SHA-256 is kept and the rest are dropped as duplicate_dropped.\n\nIf no bound record anchors validly, all copies are kept. The tie-breaker for collapse is lexicographic comparison of the lowercase hex SHA-256. A kept revocation with a revoked_by field other than \"principal\" is ignored.\n\nThe ignored-revocation rule is ambiguous in its placement within the triage flow. It could apply during 6.0 authentication or after triage keeps a record. If applied during triage, the revocation is not kept for later steps, so it would not participate in 6.4 collapse.\n\nThe ignored-revocation rule is resolved as part of 6.0 triage, so ignored records are excluded from 6.4 collapse and later steps. Only records that pass triage proceed to section 7 anchor checks. The first failure in 7.4-7.6 determines the disposition; a missing height or block hash that is not exactly 64 lowercase hex characters yields disposition `anchor_incomplete` and reason `anchor_incomplete`.\n\nA height below the lower bound yields disposition `anchor_below_lower_bound`. A height beyond the tip or absent from the view yields `anchor_beyond_observed_chain`. A block hash mismatch yields disposition `orphaned` with no reason, yet the height still counts for finality. Because section 7 only runs after the view passes rules 1-8, a missing lower bound is impossible here. Proof and batch leg checks follow in sections 7.5 and 7.6; all must pass for acceptance.\n\nAccepted records are sorted by height and record SHA-256. The event set is built from these accepted records, and the walk proceeds only if the settlement did not stop early; an underspecified anchor verdict still processes accepted records but clears deviations.\n\nSection 11.1 cross-checks only when nenrin_records is non-empty. Walks that are not strict are excluded. Other objects are indexed by record SHA-256, and each kept execution is looked up by its nenrin_ref.\n\nIf a kept execution's nenrin_ref names a walk whose contract_sha256 differs from the settlement's contract_sha256, the deviation is evidence_names_other_contract. If the ref names a walk that was excluded for not being strict, the reason is walk_not_strict. A null or absent nenrin_ref does not name any walk.\n\nExecutions set aside by 6.0, rejected by 6.6, or dropped as duplicates are not examined further. Orphaned executions are still checked. The NENRIN cross-check is skipped entirely when the settlement stops early due to a 4.1, 4.2, or 5.1 failure, so no walk_not_strict deviations arise from those records. A 6.2 input_too_large error also halts all further examination.\n\nSection 11.2 adds `evidence_missing` to accepted executions whose `nenrin_ref` is not exactly 64 lowercase hex when `requirements.evidence` is `\"nenrin_required\"`. This check runs only after section 12 confirms the record is within_grant or carries a deviation. It does not apply if the verdict is underspecified or the event set is empty. The `requirements.evidence` key is not treated as out of scope.\n\nDeviation codes and underspecified reasons are both sorted sets. The verdict is underspecified only when both are non-empty; in that case deviation_codes is empty and the reasons are listed. The JSON output must be exactly one object with the eight fields from section 9.1, using that order.\n\nEach event_set item uses the key order kind, sha256, height. Disposition keys are sorted by SHA-256 to ensure deterministic JSON output.\n\nThe output will use compact JSON with sorted keys, matching Python's default json.dumps format.\n\nWhen the contract is not a JSON object, the bond field is absent and the output uses \"n/a\". The eight-field JSON object is still required even if no further computation occurs.\n\nThe phrase \"nothing else is computed\" applies only to the two special cases where the door refuses the contract or events is not a list. In both cases, contract_sha256 is still computed and set to null.\n\nThe contract check takes precedence over the events check. If the contract is not a JSON object, the settlement returns immediately with only the `contract_not_verified` reason and no further processing.\n\nA failure in any of the first eight view rules yields exactly one reason: chain_view_rejected. Rule 9 may add view_not_strict separately. If the view is not an object or its headers are not a list, only chain_view_rejected applies; view_not_strict does not.\n\nEach header hex string must be exactly 160 lowercase hex characters, optionally followed by a single line feed. Heights must be consecutive in list order. The second header's bytes 4 through 36, reversed as hex, must equal the previous header's hash. The header structure is an 80-byte block beginning with a four-byte version field.\n\nThe 80-byte header layout is: version (4 bytes), previous block hash (bytes 4–36, reversed for display but not for anchor use), merkle root (bytes 36–68, left as-is), timestamp (bytes 68–72), nBits little-endian (bytes 72–76), and nonce (bytes 76–80). The header hash is sha256(sha256(80 bytes)), byte-reversed, and rendered as lowercase hex. The second header’s bytes 4–36, reversed as hex, must equal the previous header’s hash, following standard Bitcoin block linkage.\n\nThe nBits field decodes to a target. The header hash must be less than or equal to that target. If the grant structure is invalid, rule 1 fails and the reason is chain_view_rejected.\n\nThe lower bound is an object with an integer height and a 64-character lowercase hex hash. Other keys in the view are ignored.\n\nRule 9 requires strictness: no control characters or DEL in strings or keys, every key is ASCII, and every number is an integer within ±(2^53-1).\n\nA header that is not an object, has a non-integer height, or carries a 160-character hex string fails rule 4 with chain_view_rejected. A float height also fails rule 9 as a non-strict number. The compact target formula decodes nBits by extracting the exponent and mantissa, then shifts left or right to produce the target.\n\nRule 10 finality requires max_target_bits to be eight lowercase hex characters that decode to a positive target. Domain normalization strips Unicode whitespace, lowercases the string, removes trailing dots, forbids slashes, at-signs, colons, and spaces, enforces at least two labels, and validates each label against the regex `^[a-z0-9]([a-z0-9-]*[a-z0-9])?$`.\n\nPython `str.strip()` removes all Unicode whitespace characters, not just ASCII spaces. This means domain normalization strips tabs, newlines, and other Unicode space characters during the initial strip step.\n\n`key_url` must be an HTTPS URL with a host, validated via `urllib.parse`. Regex checks are case-insensitive. The `paid` pattern excludes matches followed by `for this record` or `for the record`. The `guarantee` pattern lacks a trailing word boundary. `does_not_establish` triggers when lowercased text contains both a runtime or enforce term and a judge, liability, or fault term, plus deviat or legal.\n\nThe `does_not_establish` check requires each of the four groups to be hit by at least one string. A group can span multiple strings, but every group must appear in at least one string.\n\nObject keys in the contract must be printable ASCII. Numbers are integers within ±(2^53-1), excluding NaN, infinity, and booleans. The maximum integer is 9007199254740991.\n\nThe contract must contain exactly two party objects, one principal and one contractor. `contract_id` and `nonce` are 32 lowercase hex characters; the example shows 32 characters, not 64. When present and non-null, `payload_digest` is 64 lowercase hex. The `grant` object is allowed to include `limits`, but if that key is present with a value, the result is undefined.\n\nOut-of-scope fields such as `limits` or `approval_policy.approvers` will trigger an error exit. The program will not emit a false settlement.\n\nThe program will exit with code 2 and an error for out-of-scope fields, since the specification forbids claiming conformance. The `spine` and `convergence` keys inside `requirements`, even when null, are also out of scope.\n\n`requirements.admission` is out of scope when present and non-null. `grant.limits` is out of scope when present and non-null. A well-formed `approval_policy.approvers` list is carried and out of scope; a malformed one is refused under rule 4.1.\n\nA well-formed approver list on the parent contract does not exempt it from rule 4.1 checks. The parent must still pass those checks, including that approvers is either absent or well-formed. Only the child settlement is undefined when it carries such a list.\n\nRule 4.1 accepts well-formed approvers on the parent contract without refusing it. The child settlement will skip the approver gate entirely when that list is present, and will not emit a settlement if the child carries one. Rule 4.2 returns unscoped_approvals_refused when allow_unscoped is truthy.\n\nRule 15 requires signatures to be a list. Each entry must be an object. Domain normalization must yield a party domain, or the signature is refused. A signature that fails verification, even for the same party, causes refusal. Every party must have at least one verifying signature.\n\nThe signature algorithm field is ignored. Rule 6 validates the key as a 32-byte canonical base64 string, but the actual point check happens during verification. The signing bytes are the ASCII prefix `a2a-contract-v0\\n` followed by the canonical serialization of the contract with its `signatures` key removed.\n\nRule 4.2 rejects contracts where two witnesses share a name or where any two party keys decode to the same 32 bytes. It also rejects contracts with more than 32 witnesses. Grant action names in authorized, prohibited, and conditional entries must match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_.:-]{0,63}$`. The tie_policy rule requires same_height to be null or `\"conservative\"`, so any other non-null value triggers tie_policy_refused.\n\nRule 4.2 flags `ack_window_missing` when `effective_at` is `delivery_ack` but `ack_window` is not an integer at least one. Rule 4.2 also flags `finality_policy_missing` when `depth` is missing or not an integer at least one. Missing `finality` passes rule 4.1 because the key is optional, so rule 4.2 adds the missing policy reason. The specification does not clarify whether a null `finality` counts as present for rule 10.\n\nA null `finality` passes rule 4.1 but still triggers `finality_policy_missing` in rule 4.2 because `depth` is missing. If `finality` is absent entirely, the view check fails and both `chain_view_rejected` and `finality_policy_missing` are returned. The `revocation_policy` row in rule 4.2 cannot fire because rule 4.1 already requires `effective_at` to be `anchor` or `delivery_ack` whenever `revocation` is an object.\n\nRule 4.2 cannot flag bad_expiry_height, authorized_actions_missing, or witness_key_invalid because rule 4.1 already rejects those cases. The bond_malformed check in rule 4.3 requires the bond amount to be an integer between 1 and 2^53-1; a boolean value like true fails this.\n\nA bond is absent or null when the key is missing or its value is null. A malformed bond is an object with a truthy amount that fails the integer range check. The outcome is undetermined for truthy amounts that are not integers, such as true, strings, or decimals. It is n/a for falsy amounts like zero.\n\nA bond is n/a when its value is not an object, such as an empty list or a string. A malformed bond is an object with a truthy amount that fails the integer range check; Python treats lists and dictionaries as truthy, so `{\"amount\": []}` or `{\"amount\": {}}` are malformed and undetermined. Null, false, empty string, and zero are falsy, so they are n/a.\n\nThe outcome is undetermined when the verdict is underspecified, pending_finality for provisional grants, held for within_grant or nothing_to_settle, and forfeited for deviation. A malformed bond always produces an underspecified verdict, so the well-formed path is never reached.\n\nParent narrowing 4.3.1 requires the child's `parent_contract` key to be an object whose `contract_id` matches the parent's. The parent must pass 4.1 on its own; if it fails, the child gets `parent_narrowing_failed` and the subset check is skipped. Both grants have already passed rule 10.\n\nThe four grant subset checks in rule 4.3.1 compare the child against the parent. Absent or null lists count as empty. A child authorized action missing from the parent is rejected. A parent prohibited action missing from the child is rejected. A child data_access entry missing from the parent is rejected. A parent max_hops integer that the child lacks or exceeds by one or more is rejected.\n\nA parent with max_hops 0 rejects every child, because the child would need a negative or absent max_hops. A child delegation allowed entry missing from the parent is rejected; if the parent lacks delegation, any child allowed entry fails. A conditional entry in the parent is complex. A child conditional action must appear in the parent's authorized or conditional actions. A parent expiry_height integer that the child lacks or exceeds by one or more is rejected. Revocation narrowing, finality narrowing, and witness key string checks follow.\n\nRules 13 and 14 compare allow_unscoped and approvers between parent and child. The conditional subset rule skips a parent entry if its action is prohibited or absent from the child's authorized and conditional actions. Otherwise it requires the child to have a matching conditional entry with the same requires value, or the action must be absent from the child entirely.\n\nA child authorized action that is absent from its conditional entries still triggers a violation if the parent has a matching conditional entry. The requires value is compared as null when missing, so a parent with a requires string and a child without one fails. Rule 9 narrows revocation: an anchor effective_at on the parent must match an anchor on the child; a delivery_ack may match either anchor or delivery_ack. If both are delivery_ack, the child's ack_window must be an integer less than or equal to the parent's.\n\nRule 10 requires the child to have a finality object when the parent does. If the parent specifies an integer depth, the child must provide an integer depth at least as large. The child's max_target_bits must decode and yield a target no larger than the parent's. If the parent omits max_target_bits, that axis is not compared.\n\nRule 11 requires the child to have at least one witness for every Ed25519 public key string present in the parent, and every such key must match exactly. Rule 12 compares privacy and ordering separately; when the parent sets either, the child must set it to the identical JSON value, with object key order ignored.\n\nApprovers are compared as sets of canonical bytes, with absent entries treated as empty lists. The order of actions within each entry matters for canonicalization. When the parent has approvers and the child does not, the child is rejected. For revocation effective height, an anchor effective_at maps to the current block height.\n\nFor a delivery_ack effective_at, the revocation height is the minimum of the current block height plus ack_window and the heights of every accepted acknowledgement that names this revocation. If no acknowledgements exist, the height equals h + ack_window. An acknowledgement names a revocation if its revocation_sha256 matches the lowercase hex body digest of the revocation, or if its record_sha256 matches a copy still present in the input after step 6.4.\n\nThe body digest is the SHA-256 of the canonical record with the anchor removed, including signatures. It is formatted as lowercase hex. After step 6.4, only kept copies remain in the input; if none are validly anchored, every copy is kept and any can name a revocation. A delivery_ack anchored before the revocation lowers its effective height below h. Revocations are sorted by effective height and record hash. Execution at block height h is revoked if any revocation has an effective height less than or equal to h.\n\nA revoked execution yields deviation revoked for every string in performed_actions. No further checks are applied to that record, including expiry, prohibited actions, conditional rules, unauthorized actions, and delegation. However, approvals carried by an accepted execution are still classified even when the execution is revoked. Any approval judged as forged adds deviation forged_approval.\n\nFor revoked executions, conditional actions are skipped and their approval nonces are not consumed. Section 10.2 applies expiry checks: if an execution's height exceeds its expiry_height, every action string produces after_expiry, though this does not halt other checks; an expired prohibited action yields both after_expiry and prohibited. Section 10.3 processes each unrevoked execution's actions in order: prohibited actions are flagged as prohibited, conditional actions proceed to section 10.4, and all remaining actions are treated as unauthorized. Any delegated_to entry missing from the delegation allowed list is flagged as a delegation deviation.\n\nEvery approval on an accepted execution is classified. A non-object or missing principal key is forged. Valid approvals carry a valid_until_height integer, a 32-character lowercase hex nonce, a boolean single_use flag, and a 64-character lowercase hex contract_sha256. The verification message is the canonical bytes of a map containing those fields, prefixed with the string \"a2a-approval-v2\". Ed25519 signature verification against the principal key determines the outcome: if the signature is valid and the contract_sha256 matches the current contract hash, the approval is counted; otherwise it is labeled other_terms. If the signature does not verify against the current form but does verify against an older form, the label is bound.\n\nIf the v2 signature does not verify, the code falls back to older forms. The v1 form applies only when the approval fields pass checks except for contract_sha256. It uses the contract's id and payload_digest rather than the approval's own values. The v0 form canonicalizes a map containing contract_id, payload_digest, and action. If none of these older forms verify, the approval is classified as forged.\n\nIf the contract lacks a payload_digest, the fallback canonical map uses null for that field. The approval's action is also canonicalized as null when absent. In the v2 form, all fields are drawn directly from the approval object.\n\nIf the approval reaches step 2, its fields are present and valid. The signature base64 string is either 64 bytes and strict, or missing; a non-strict signature would have been rejected earlier. A missing signature or failed verification marks the approval as forged. For conditional approvals, the approval's action field must match the execution's action.\n\nApprovals are consumed in event set order by height and SHA, then by list order within each record. The first approval that satisfies all four criteria is used. If single_use is true, its nonce is consumed and shared across all actions and executions. Nonces are not consumed for revoked executions because conditional actions there are skipped, though approvals are still classified to detect forged ones. Whether a revoked execution's approval can be reused by a later unrevoked execution remains unclear.\n\nCounted approvals from revoked records remain valid and can be consumed by later executions. Revocation does not invalidate approvals already on accepted records.\n\nThe implementation will use a three-pass algorithm. First, classify every approval on all accepted executions and collect forged deviations. Second, compute revocation effective heights and mark revoked executions, adding their deviations. Third, process the remaining checks in event set order.\n\nFor single-use approvals, the search must proceed in event set order. An approval is valid only if it appears on the same record or on a record at a strictly lower height. Approvals from the same block with a different SHA are not considered. The first matching approval is consumed if it is single-use.\n\nApprovals later in the same record's list can still approve actions, because they belong to the same record. A single-use approval is consumed by the first action that matches it; subsequent actions in the same record cannot reuse it.\n\nConditional actions do not need to appear in authorized_actions. If an action is both prohibited and conditional, the prohibited check takes precedence and the conditional check is skipped. When the delegation key is absent, the allowed list is empty and any delegated_to entry produces a deviation. If delegated_to is missing entirely, no delegation deviations are generated. The performed_actions field must be a list of strings for strict body validation when present.\n\nA missing performed_actions field is a conformance problem under section 6.6, since it is not a list of strings. If the contractor signature verifies, the execution is classified as a charge and continues to section 7. The absence of performed_actions may prevent iterating over acts, though the record's acts are still judged.\n\nA performed_actions list longer than 256 strings is a conformance problem. NENRIN 11.1 indexes walks by the canonical record_sha256 of the walk object; if that walk is not strict, an execution whose nenrin_ref matches it receives the reason walk_not_strict.\n\nMultiple walk issues are collected as a set so each reason appears once. The evidence_names_other_contract deviation applies only when a strict indexed walk is found and the execution context object contains a non-null contract_sha256 that differs from the current contract hash; missing or absent fields produce no deviation. If nenrin_ref does not match any walk, section 11.1 generates no deviation. It remains unclear whether section 11.1 checks executions kept by section 6.0 or only those left after section 6.4, including orphaned records and those failing anchors.\n\nSection 7 checks apply to all executions kept by section 6.0, not only orphaned ones; the parenthetical exception covers only orphaned records. If the algorithm stops early before reaching section 7, those checks are skipped. Duplicate records are removed after section 6.4, so each remaining execution has a unique SHA-256.\n\nRule 7.6 governs batch leg operations. If no hexlify operation exists, the batch is accepted. Otherwise, hexlify must be the first operation and appear only once; any other placement or multiple occurrences produce a hexlify_not_leading deviation. The batch is then rebuilt from the operations following the first hexlify up to the first sha256. Each intervening operation must be an append or prepend; if none appears, the batch is malformed. The reconstructed batch concatenates all prepend operands in front, followed by the ASCII lowercase hex of the body digest, followed by all append operands in order.\n\nPrepend operands are reversed so the last one appears at the front of the reconstructed batch. The final batch bytes must be UTF-8 text that parses as strict JSON into an object matching one of the three batch schemas.\n\nIf the reconstructed batch bytes fail to parse as strict JSON, or if they parse but are not a strict object, the deviation is batch_not_json. If the parsed object matches none of the three allowed schemas, the deviation is batch_schema_unknown. The records field must be a list of objects.\n\nThe batch count must equal the number of records. Each record's SHA-256 must match the hex digest exactly once; otherwise the record is either missing or listed twice. For typed batches, each record's kind must map to its schema, and any record with a schema mismatch produces a record_schema_mismatch deviation.\n\nThe reconstructed batch text must contain the raw 64-character hex SHA-256 of each record at the byte offset equal to the total length of all prepend operands. Escaped characters or an incorrect offset produce a batch_leg_splice_not_at_listing deviation.\n\nA custom JSON parser will record the byte offset and raw length of each string value, and flag whether it contains escapes. This avoids ambiguity when locating a record's SHA-256 in the raw batch text.\n\nThe parser will check for escaped characters before verifying the SHA-256 offset. The strict JSON rule applies to batch bytes, but duplicate keys in the reconstructed batch are not treated as a settlement error; they only abort input validation.\n\nBatch parse failures, including duplicate keys, oversized integers, and non-strict JSON, will produce a batch_not_json deviation. Settlement only aborts for input file parse errors.\n\nWitness and trace-pin batches are untyped, so step 7 is skipped. The SHA-256 digest must be lowercase hex. Proof operations are sha256, hexlify, append hex, and prepend hex. The current byte buffer may not exceed 65536 bytes at any step. The proof list must contain at most 512 operations.\n\nThe final proof bytes must equal the Merkle root from bytes 36–68 of the raw header. A missing anchor.proof produces an anchor_proof_invalid deviation. Anchor strictness requires hex values to be lowercase pairs; uppercase or malformed hex makes the anchor non-strict and prevents reaching section 7.5. Missing hex keys on append operations produce a malformed operand error. Extra keys in proof operation objects are not explicitly forbidden, so they may be allowed.\n\nExtra keys in proof operations are ignored. The hex field is omitted for sha256 and hexlify operations. The body digest is the SHA-256 of the canonical record without its anchor key, including signatures. Record signing bytes are the context prefix plus the canonical record with signatures and anchor removed. Valid contexts are a2a-execution-v0, a2a-revocation-v0, and a2a-revocation-ack-v0. A signature is strictly verified when one entry has a role allowed by the schema, verifies with the matching key over the signing bytes, and is itself a strict value.\n\nExecution signatures require a contractor key or a witness key with the exact witness name. Revocation signatures require a principal key. Acknowledgement signatures require a contractor key. The signatures field must be a non-empty list. A record is still strictly signed if at least one entry is valid and the rest are invalid.\n\nThe exact meaning of \"a signature verifies\" for lax signatures is unclear. It may mean any entry verifies under 3.4 alone, or only entries whose keys match an allowed role.\n\nA signature is strictly verified only when an entry with an allowed role verifies under 3.4 with the matching key and the entry itself is a strict value. A malleated signature may verify under lax OpenSSL but fail strict checks, so it does not count as strictly signed.\n\nA signature that verifies under 3.4 but fails strictness is set aside as signature_not_strict rather than rejected. The case where the verifying signature uses a wrong role is still being considered.\n\nA signature that verifies under 3.4 but fails strictness is set aside as signature_not_strict. If no allowed-role entry verifies, the record is rejected. A witness name must match exactly; otherwise the entry has no key and is skipped. A bad point in the key causes unusable rather than true. Multiple witnesses with the same name produce a key_conflict deviation.\n\nWitness lookup returns the first match when 4.1 fails. Principal and contractor keys come from the parties object. Malformed parties, missing keys, or non-strict signing reject the record unless lax verification applies. Body strictness also fails if nenrin_ref is neither null nor 64 lowercase hex, contract_ref fields are not 64 lowercase hex, or execution performed_actions is not a list of strings.\n\nExecution body strictness also requires delegated_to to be a list of strings and approvals to be a list of objects. An approval is invalid if its signature decodes to 64 bytes but fails strictness. A null value for nenrin_ref or contract_ref fields is acceptable, yet an explicit null key on contract_ref fields would still fail body strictness because null is not 64 lowercase hex.\n\nA null contract_ref or absent contract_sha256 is valid binding, but if the record is strictly signed it becomes record_not_strict. If the record is not strictly signed and body strictness fails, it is set aside without reason. A non-object contract_ref prevents checking its fields.\n\nA missing anchor is strict by default. If present, it is non-strict when its value is not a strict value, block_hash is not exactly 64 lowercase hex, or any proof operation’s hex operand is not an even-length sequence of lowercase hex digits. An absent height produces an anchor_incomplete deviation.\n\nThe record SHA-256 is the digest of the canonical record. In binding, duplicate records with identical SHA-256 are merged and the first occurrence wins. The events directory holds one element per file, but settlement order carries no meaning; binding still processes files in their given order.\n\nFilenames will be sorted to ensure deterministic order. A missing events directory yields an empty list, and a non-list nenrin value is treated as absent. parent.json is optional. Files are read as UTF-8; parse errors exit with code 2. Unknown files are ignored. On success the program emits JSON and exits with code 0.\n\nCanonical JSON string encoding will escape quotes, backslashes, and control characters. It will use lowercase \\u escapes for unprintable characters. Lone surrogate code points will be preserved in strings rather than combined into paired surrogates.\n\nCanonical JSON will use lowercase \\udxxx escapes for lone surrogates. A custom parser is needed because Python's json.loads mishandles raw surrogates. Object keys sort by Unicode code point. Canonical output contains no whitespace. Integers omit leading zeros and use a minus sign for negatives. The parser must distinguish integers from floats.\n\nIntegers outside ±(2^53-1) are parsed as integers but fail strictness and cause rejection under rule 11. Python int handles arbitrarily large values, so canonical output can represent them. The 4300-digit limit is the parse error threshold. Floats are stored as Python float; 1e400 becomes infinity, and NaN, Infinity, and -Infinity are preserved.\n\nA custom Number class will tag integers and floats so strict checks can distinguish them without Python's isinstance(True, int) quirk. Canonical JSON will walk this tagged structure.\n\nThe parser will reject duplicate keys and empty files. It will also reject UTF-8 BOM. The Ed25519 implementation is now being written with the correct curve constants, base point, and point encoding.\n\nEd25519 point decoding reads 32 little-endian bytes, clears the top bit to recover y, and rejects if y is not strictly less than p. The x coordinate is recovered from y using the standard curve equation and square-root recovery; if the first candidate fails, the point is flipped by the imaginary unit I. A sign mismatch between the recovered x parity and the original top bit is corrected by negating x. Canonical encoding is validated by re-encoding and comparing.\n\nThe identity point is encoded as 0x01 followed by 31 zero bytes. Prime order will be verified by multiplying a point by the curve order L and checking that the result is the identity. Edwards addition will be implemented in extended twisted Edwards coordinates.\n\nThe Edwards addition formulas for extended coordinates are now fixed. Scalar multiplication will use double-and-add, which is acceptable for the 253-bit curve order. Signature verification will compute k as SHA512(R || A || message) mod L and check that [S]B equals R + [k]A.\n\nRFC 8032 requires S < L for Ed25519 verification. A signature with S >= L returns false rather than unusable. Unusable is reserved for 3.2 failures: bad base64, wrong length, non-canonical base64, invalid point, identity, or non-prime-order key. A signature with an invalid R is not treated as unusable.\n\nBase64 canonicality requires decoding and re-encoding to yield identical text. Standard base64 with padding is used. Missing padding, non-alphabet characters, or incorrect padding bits are rejected.\n\nThe base64 canonicality check now explicitly compares the decoded bytes against the expected length. Signature strictness requires the lower 32 bytes to decode as a valid public key that is neither the identity point nor of non-prime order, and the upper 32 bytes must be strictly less than the curve order L.\n\nA signature with S >= L is rejected rather than set aside as signature_not_strict. The test vectors synth_signature_s_plus_l and synth_strict_signature_beside_malleated are designed to exercise this strictness rule.\n\nThe reference implementation uses OpenSSL via the Python cryptography package, which may accept malleated signatures with S >= L. The specification notes that while libraries disagree on lax signatures, they all agree on strict ones.\n\nNOTES.md N9 documents that OpenSSL alone may accept non-strict signatures as valid. The strictness check therefore sits on top of OpenSSL verification, so a signature accepted by OpenSSL but failing the strict threshold is not strictly verified. The specification defines \"verifies\" as RFC 8032 returning true.\n\nThe specification distinguishes \"does not verify\" from \"strictly verifies\": the former applies to both false and unusable, while the latter requires both a strict signature and RFC 8032 returning true. Because RFC 8032 already rejects S >= L, the strictness check for S is redundant in that case; however, the R point check remains necessary because RFC 8032 accepts any curve point including small-order points.\n\nThe v1.16 test vectors reveal that a malleated signature with S >= L can trigger both contract_not_verified and contract_signature_not_strict, because RFC 8032 rejects it while OpenSSL might accept it. Without expected outputs, the correct behavior must be inferred from the specification alone.\n\nThe vector `synth_signature_s_plus_l` tests that a signature with S equal to or greater than the curve order L is rejected as non-strict. The vector `synth_strict_signature_beside_malleated` likely pairs a valid strict signature with a malleated one, and the specification says that extra entries in a signatures list that do not verify or are not strict are ignored.\n\nA record with one valid strict signature and an extra malleated entry remains strictly signed because the good signature counts. The bad entry is ignored. For contracts, an extra malleated signature that does not verify still refuses the contract even if another party has a good signature. If OpenSSL accepts the malleated S+L signature as valid, it is treated as non-strict.\n\nThe specification explicitly ties verification to RFC 8032, which rejects signatures with S >= L. This means a malleated signature cannot be accepted by the contract door while still being flagged as non-strict. The remaining puzzle is reconciling this with the reference implementation's use of OpenSSL.\n\nThe specification explicitly ties verification to RFC 8032, not lax OpenSSL. The strictness check is additional and required for R to be a canonical prime-order non-identity point. Because RFC 8032 already rejects signatures with S >= L, the S < L threshold is redundant in that case; only the R point check remains necessary.\n\nRFC 8032's cofactorless equation check rejects signatures whose R point contains a torsion component, because [8]R would not satisfy the curve equation. OpenSSL may accept such signatures under cofactored verification. This confirms that RFC 8032 is the correct verification standard.\n\nRFC 8032 permits either the cofactorless equation [S]B = R + [k]A or the cofactored equation [8][S]B = [8]R + [8][k]A. Both are acceptable for verification, and libraries may differ on which they use. For strict signatures, both checks agree because R and A lie in the prime-order subgroup and S is below L. The implementation will perform point decoding and apply the S < L threshold, but it will not enforce a prime-order check on the decoded points.\n\nPublic keys that fail the prime-order check are rejected as unusable before verification begins. Because all valid keys are prime order, the only remaining source of disagreement between cofactored and cofactorless verification is the R point. A signature is strictly verified only when R is also prime order.\n\nCofactorless verification makes strict and verify nearly identical, because a non-prime-order R almost never satisfies the equation. The signature_not_strict disposition therefore applies only when verify returns true but the entry is not strictly valid: either the R point fails the prime-order test while the cofactored equation holds, or the JSON value is malformed.\n\nA malformed signature entry that still verifies yields a signature_not_strict disposition. Cofactorless verification alone would reduce such cases.\n\nThe specification's normative text requires RFC 8032 verification, yet the reference implementation uses OpenSSL. The test vectors capture the reference's actual behavior, so the implementation must match that behavior rather than only the written standard. A strict signature must verify against the reference's OpenSSL-based logic.\n\nThe disposition of a signature with S equal to or greater than the curve order is still unclear without the vector. OpenSSL 1.1.1 originally accepted malleated signatures because it did not check S against L, and a CVE was later fixed. OpenSSL 3.0+ may still accept such signatures in some versions.\n\nThe specification dates to 2026-10-17, which aligns with cryptography 50.0.1. OpenSSL 3.4 may have added S < L checks, but the normative text requires RFC 8032 verification. I am re-examining RFC 8032 section 2 to confirm whether it mandates rejecting signatures with S >= L.\n\nRFC 8032 explicitly requires rejecting signatures when the second half decodes to an integer S that is out of range, including cases where S is equal to or greater than the curve order L. This confirms that the strictness threshold is already normative within the RFC itself.\n\nI am reconsidering whether RFC 8032 actually mandates the cofactored verification equation, or if the cofactorless form is the primary requirement. I will examine how Python's cryptography and OpenSSL actually implement Ed25519 verification to determine the correct approach.\n\nThe specification's triage table explicitly lists a \"lax\" case where a signature verifies under 3.4 alone but is not strictly signed. This means the implementation must accept S >= L in verify() to produce signature_not_strict dispositions, even though RFC 8032 normally rejects those signatures. The implementation will therefore use the cofactorless equation check and enforce the S < L threshold in verify(), while still allowing the signature_not_strict path.\n\nI will implement verify strictly per RFC 8032, rejecting signatures with S >= L. A signature whose S equals or exceeds the curve order will be treated as not strictly signed and will fail verification. If the body is strict, the record is rejected; if it is a contract, the contract is refused and carries both contract_not_verified and contract_signature_not_strict dispositions. This aligns with the written specification text, though it may conflict with any vector expecting OpenSSL lax acceptance.\n\nThe implementation will use the cofactorless equation [S]B = R + [k]A with the S < L threshold. Non-canonical R points, including non-canonical y coordinates, will be rejected during decoding.\n\nNon-canonical public keys with the high bit set will be rejected. Small-order points are also unusable because the curve order L is odd, so 8 does not divide L and [L]P cannot equal the identity for them.\n\nThe prime-order check will use [L]P == O, with identity excluded separately. A fast [8]P != O shortcut was considered but rejected because mixed-order points satisfy the cofactorless equation yet fail the full order check.\n\nI will embed the RFC 8032 test vector for self-checking. The program will remain a single file with no external dependencies.\n\nNo runtime self-check will be added. SHA-512 is available in hashlib. For batch leg JSON parsing, offsets must be tracked because the SHA-256 string is hex ASCII and its byte offset equals its UTF-8 offset.\n\nThe raw 64-character hex string must be written exactly as it appears in the JSON text. The parser will track byte offsets and escape flags to extract that exact slice for each record. Duplicate SHA values are already rejected during step 6, so each record maps to a unique hex string. The batch implementation will build the byte stream, decode UTF-8, parse JSON with strictness and offset tracking, then validate schema fields, records, count, and unique SHA matching.\n\nThe implementation will distinguish between internal and batch_leg_splice_not_at_listing dispositions by inspecting the matched SHA string's raw JSON encoding. If the string uses escapes, the disposition is internal. Otherwise, if the first character's byte offset does not equal the pre_len insertion point, the disposition is batch_leg_splice_not_at_listing. This ensures the splice is inserted exactly at the start of the SHA value and not inside another string or after a partial escape.\n\nThe first hex character must sit at the pre_len insertion point, so the opening quote is exactly one byte before that boundary. Each header line is 160 lowercase hex characters followed by a single optional line feed; uppercase hex or CRLF fails rule 4. The 160-character hex string decodes to 80 bytes, and it is unclear whether the trailing line feed is stripped before hashing.\n\nThe optional line feed is stripped before hashing, so rules 1-8 pass while rule 9 rejects the line feed. Heights must be non-negative integers, and consecutive headers must increment by exactly one.\n\nA view holds the header at lower_bound.height whose hash equals lower_bound.hash. Heights are consecutive, so each height maps to exactly one header. The tip is the height of the last header.\n\nIf the view verifies but contains more than 200,000 headers, the disposition is input_too_large and no record is examined. This does not also add chain_view_rejected. Rule 9 still applies independently and may produce view_not_strict alongside input_too_large.\n\nTriage occurs before the view check per section 2.3, so triage dispositions already stand when examination stops. The input_too_large disposition is separate and also halts further processing.\n\nThe input_too_large threshold for records applies only to those kept by the triage table. Ignored, set aside, and rejected records are excluded from that count.\n\nThe 10,000-record limit counts only records that survive the full 6.0 authentication stage. Records set aside, rejected, or ignored are excluded from this count. Duplicate copies of a single record collapse to one entry during 6.1 merging before triage. Foreign, inconsistent, and unbound records are not bound, triaged, or counted.\n\nSection 9.2 clarifies that bound records never examined because sections 4, 5, or 6.2 failed carry no disposition. Triage dispositions are assigned before the 4.1 check, so a record that fails 4.1 is not examined further and receives none.\n\nA contradiction exists between section 2.3 and section 9.2. Section 2.3 assigns set aside and rejected dispositions during triage before the section 4/5 check, yet section 9.2 states that bound records never examined because sections 4, 5, or 6.2 failed carry no disposition. The exact meaning of \"never examined\" is ambiguous: it may mean triage should be skipped when those checks fail, or that triage dispositions do not count as examination.\n\nThe apparent contradiction may be resolved by interpreting \"examined\" as meaning only sections 6.4 and beyond, not triage. If so, triage dispositions would remain valid even when later checks fail, and the reference order might differ from the paragraph order.\n\nThe paragraph may be a summary rather than a strict execution order. Triage runs before the contract and view checks, so it completes even if those fail. The phrase \"no record is examined further\" then applies only to steps after this paragraph, not to triage itself. This resolves the contradiction: triage dispositions remain valid when sections 4, 5, or 6.2 fail.\n\nReasons from 6.0 failures are collected even when the record walk never runs, because they do not stop event set computation. If 4.1 fails, the settlement is underspecified with contract_not_verified and the event set is empty, yet those reasons may still be included.\n\nRe-reading 9.2 resolves the earlier contradiction: bound records are not examined when sections 4, 5, or 6.2 fail, so triage is skipped entirely and no dispositions are assigned.\n\nA third reading of section 9.2 reconciles the contradiction: records that would have been kept and examined further receive no disposition when the computation stops early, but set-aside and rejected records from step 6.0 still retain their dispositions even if sections 4, 5, or 6.2 fail.\n\nIf the view check fails, anchor_not_strict reasons and dispositions still apply because anchors validly does not depend on section 4. If the view passes but section 4.1 fails, anchors validly can still be checked for triage table row 5. Section 6.4 is only reached when section 5 rules 1-8 pass, even if section 4 failed.\n\nIf 4.1 fails but the view passes, the triage table may still set aside a bad anchor copy while leaving valid copies untouched; those valid copies receive no disposition because section 6.4 is skipped. If both 4.1 and the view fail, anchors validly is false and non-strict strictly signed records get the anchor_not_strict reason and disposition. The question remains whether 6.0 reasons are collected when 4.1 fails.\n\nThe event set is empty when the record walk does not run, even if reasons from 6.0 or other sections are collected. Section 9.1 clarifies that those reasons alone do not empty the set; the emptiness is caused by the failed walk. Section 6.2 then adds input_too_large and halts further examination when the kept count exceeds 10,000.\n\nThe likely execution order is: compute the contract hash, check sections 4 and 5, bind records, then triage. If sections 4 or 5 fail, no record dispositions are assigned because triage is skipped. Section 6.2 is checked during record examination after some records are kept.\n\nSection 5 includes rule 9, yet that rule does not stop the walk. Therefore \"sections 4, 5\" in section 9.2 refers only to the hard stop conditions: 4.1, 4.2, 5.1 rules 1-8, header input_too_large, and 6.2. A failed rule 9 alone does not prevent record examination, so bound records still receive dispositions when only that rule fails.\n\nForeign, inconsistent, and unbound records retain their binding dispositions even when examination stops early, because binding is not the same as examining anchors. The remaining puzzle is whether 6.0 reasons are collected at all when triage is skipped.\n\nThe implementation will parse inputs first, then check the contract object, compute the SHA-256 hash, verify out-of-scope status, and run sections 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, and 5.1.\n\nThe concrete execution order is now set. After binding events, the code checks hard stop conditions. If any fail, bound records are skipped and no triage dispositions are assigned. Otherwise triage runs to assign set-aside, rejected, and ignored dispositions while collecting 6.0 reasons. The kept count is then checked against 10,000; if exceeded, input_too_large halts further examination. Finally, collapse, conformance, anchor validation, event set construction, walk, and nenrin proceed.\n\n4.2 reasons are a hard stop condition that empties the event set and leaves the verdict underspecified. 4.3 reasons do not halt examination. A missing finality depth therefore prevents any record review even when valid anchored records exist, because horizon computation requires depth. View rules may also depend on depth.\n\nAll 4.2 reasons halt examination and leave the event set empty. 4.3 reasons do not stop the walk; the event set is still built and dispositions are assigned, but the verdict becomes underspecified and deviations are cleared. Whether the walk's own deviations are cleared remains an open question.\n\nSection 12 confirms that an underspecified verdict empties deviations. The NENRIN walk should still run to collect its own underspecified reasons, such as walk_not_strict, because those reasons do not stop event set construction. Evidence names from other contracts are cleared as deviations when the verdict is underspecified, but evidence_missing is only cleared if the final verdict would be within_grant or deviation.\n\nThe walk and its checks are collected even when 4.2 halts further examination. An underspecified status requires no horizon calculation; otherwise the finality depth is an integer of at least one, so the view tip exists and the lower bound height is valid. Orphaned heights count toward status. The bond outcome is undetermined for well-formed or truthy malformed bonds, and n/a otherwise. Contract SHA-256 must also handle non-integer numbers.\n\nCanonical JSON must be writable for signing bytes, so 4.1 rule 11 will reject non-canonical contracts even though the SHA-256 hash is still computed. Duplicate keys and depth values above 512 are parse errors that halt settlement before computation begins. Arrays and null contracts are not objects and yield contract_not_verified with a null hash. The now-established regex patterns will be implemented in Python using case-insensitive matching.\n\nThe establishes regex patterns now include payment, contract, binding, guarantee, escrow, custody, solvent, lawful, fair, and certified. A record is refused if any establishes string matches any pattern. The does_not_establish list must be non-empty and contain only strings; otherwise the record is refused.\n\nThe establishes list must be non-empty and contain only strings. Non-string elements are refused because they do not match the forbidden patterns.\n\nFor the establishes list, only string elements will be checked against the forbidden patterns; non-string entries will not trigger a refusal. The key_url check will use urllib.parse.urlparse to require an HTTPS scheme and non-empty host, but it will not verify that the host matches the contract domain.\n\nDomain normalization must succeed and yield distinct values. Labels are split on dots, require at least two segments, and each segment must match the regex `^[a-z0-9]([a-z0-9-]*[a-z0-9])?$`. This rejects empty labels, leading or trailing hyphens, and consecutive hyphens.\n\nDomain normalization strips whitespace and lowercases the address before removing trailing dots. A null value for an allowed key such as limits passes 4.1 because null is not out of scope; it is ignored by the door.\n\nAn empty `limits` object is out of scope and will be rejected before settlement. The `approval_policy.approvers` check requires an object with only `allow_unscoped` and `approvers` keys. When present, `approvers` must be a list of one to sixteen objects. Each object may contain `name`, `public_key_ed25519_b64`, and `actions`. Names and public keys must be unique. Actions must be non-empty distinct strings drawn from the conditional entry actions. If this structure passes, the child contract is out of scope.\n\nIf the approvers key is present but fails validation, the contract is in scope and marked contract_not_verified. The allow_unscoped flag must be a boolean when present; absent or false values are not truthy, so only true triggers the unscoped_approvals_refused reason under 4.2. Each conditional entry requires a non-empty string action, and the actions must be unique.\n\nConditional entries allow empty strings for the requires field. A non-string requires value triggers refusal.\n\nDelegation objects must contain exactly one allowed key with a list of non-empty strings. Empty objects and extra keys are refused.\n\nOrdering objects may only include same_height as a string or null.\n\nWitnesses require objects with a non-empty name and a public key that decodes to 32 bytes. The door does not perform the full Ed25519 point check; that validation is deferred to key_conflict.\n\nWitness signatures require the full Ed25519 point check from section 3.2.\n\nPrivacy must be a non-empty string when present.\n\nRevocation is an object with effective_at set to \"anchor\" or \"delivery_ack\", and optional ack_window as an integer of at least one. A null revocation passes rule 10 but fails the 4.2 object check, so it is set aside as revocation_not_provided_for.\n\ndata_access must be a list of non-empty strings when present. max_hops and expiry_height are integers greater than or equal to zero. finality may contain depth as an integer of at least one, and max_target_bits as eight lowercase hex digits that decode to a positive target. The schema field must be exactly \"a2a-contract-v0\".\n\nEvery object key in the contract, including nested ones, must be printable ASCII. Numbers must be integers within range; floats and big integers fail. Signatures are included in the walk and counted toward rule 12. Extra fields in conformance lists trigger a problem. Execution outcomes require a present non-null value that is not an object; each key must appear in performed_actions; each value must be ok, error, timeout, or partial.\n\nApproval objects must contain an action matching the pattern, or they count as not matching. A null outcomes list is acceptable. Non-object entries in the approvals list are caught by 6.0 type checks before reaching 6.6.\n\n6.0 only sends kept records to 6.6, so approvals is always a list of objects or absent. performed_actions must be a list of strings or absent; its absence triggers a conformance problem. A kept record with missing performed_actions is charged if the contractor signature verifies, otherwise rejected. Witness-signed executions without performed_actions are rejected because they lack a contractor signature.\n\nA kept record is strictly signed because its contractor signature verifies. A witness-signed execution without a contractor signature is rejected. If a kept record has a conformance problem, the verifying contractor signature triggers charging.\n\nRejected records are excluded from subsequent anchor checks and event sets. The settlement sequence is collapse copies, then conformance, then anchors. Duplicate-dropped records skip conformance checks entirely. Rejected records remain rejected and do not receive anchor disposition.\n\nConformance problems take precedence over anchor failures. A kept record with a conformance problem continues to anchor checks and adds the deviation nonconforming_record. If that execution then fails anchor with an underspecified reason, the verdict becomes underspecified, the deviation is cleared from output, and the disposition is the anchor failure rather than accepted. When anchor checks pass, the record is accepted and the deviation remains.\n\nAn anchor below the lower bound yields disposition and reason, not accepted, and is excluded from the event set. Other records continue processing; the event set is not emptied. The walk runs only on accepted records. If this reason exists, the verdict becomes underspecified and deviations are cleared.\n\nAn orphaned record receives disposition orphaned with no reason, is excluded from the event set, and its height still counts for finality. NENRIN still reads orphaned executions.\n\nThe height of an orphaned record falls within the view's range but its hash does not match.\n\nThe view holds headers at every consecutive height from its first header through the tip. Because lower_bound.height is within this range, no record can claim a height below the view start.\n\nThe \"view holds no header\" check is redundant once rules 1 through 8 pass, but it is kept as a defensive guard. Proof and batch failures add reasons and dispositions to individual records without rejecting them or clearing the event set; the walk continues for accepted records.\n\nThe specification lists reasons that do not empty the event set, but anchor_proof_invalid is not among them. This implies that an anchor proof failure may actually clear the event set, unlike conformance or anchor bounds problems.\n\nSection 12 specifies that one strictly signed record whose anchor fails rows one through three of 7.4, or 7.5, is the condition for a verdict.\n\nAnchor failures do not empty the event set. The walk runs over accepted records after anchor checks, so failed anchors are excluded from the event set while other records remain. Section 9.1 lists reasons that do not empty the event set because those reasons are collected without stopping the computation of the event set.\n\nDispositions include both accepted and failed-anchor records. When the verdict is underspecified due to an anchor proof failure, the event set may remain non-empty while deviation codes are cleared and the reason is recorded in underspecified_reasons. The next step is to determine whether status is computed.\n\nWhen the verdict is underspecified, status is undetermined regardless of heights. The ack body digest for a revocation is the SHA-256 of the canonical record without its anchor, encoded in lowercase hex. A revocation names an execution if its sha256 matches either the body digest or the record sha256 from the copies list. An execution is revoked by any revocation whose effective height is less than or equal to the execution height. Sorting by effective height and sha may be used only for output.\n\nThe boolean check for revocation does not depend on sorting. The authority window ends at block E inclusive. The ack window is an integer greater than or equal to one when the delivery_ack passes 4.2, but it is ignored if effective_at is anchor. Revocations are only included in the event set when they pass 4.1 validation; otherwise they were set aside in 6.0.\n\nThe principal key is usable because the contract passed 4.1 verification, so \"no principal key\" cannot apply. The canonical object for v2 must contain exactly five keys: contract_sha256, action, valid_until_height, nonce, and single_use. valid_until_height is an integer greater than or equal to zero; nonce is a 32-character lowercase hex string; single_use is a boolean; contract_sha256 is a 64-character lowercase hex string. Action may be absent from the canonical object and is treated as null.\n\nThe canonical object may contain any JSON value for action, not just a string. If the approval action is non-string, it will not match a string action but can still be counted or forged. For v1 messages, the payload_digest defaults to null when absent.\n\nThe canonical v2 object uses the approval's action, valid_until_height, nonce, and single_use. The v1 object is built from the contract's fields rather than the approval's. Signature verification follows a strict order: attempt v2 first if its fields hold; if v2 verifies, classify it as v2 and skip older forms. If v2 fails or its fields are missing, attempt v1 only if its fields hold, then attempt v0. If none of these verify, fall back to the older form.\n\nSignature verification returns true, false, or unusable. A non-string signature is unusable. Forged approvals on revoked executions still count and may be used by later records. Single-use nonce consumption only occurs when a nonce approves an action; forged or label-bound approvals do not consume it.\n\nThe consumed nonce set is global across all actions and executions. A single-use approval consumes its nonce string, blocking any other single-use approval with the same nonce. Non-single-use approvals do not consume nonces.\n\nThe approval search scans the event set in order, so a single-use approval consumed by an earlier action is unavailable to a later action on the same record. Non-single-use approvals with the same nonce remain usable.\n\nThe event set item format is a JSON object with kind, sha256, and height. Kind names map to execution, revocation, or revocation_ack. Height is an integer. Arrays and the dispositions object are sorted alphabetically. Empty arrays are emitted as empty arrays rather than null. The final JSON object is printed to stdout with a trailing newline.\n\nThe program will print a JSON error object and exit with code 2 when input is invalid. Error reasons are short strings: duplicate_key, invalid_json, nesting_too_deep, integer_too_long, and utf-8_decode_error. The canonical contract SHA-256 must exclude only the signatures key.\n\nCanonical JSON serialization sorts keys at every level. Python's repr handles floats like 1.0 and 1e2 correctly, but the specification requires integer tokens, so 1e2 is rejected as a non-integer during field checks. Values such as 1e400 become Infinity in canonical form and are not treated as strict errors. The integer 9007199254740992 exceeds the valid range.\n\nThe parser will reject leading zeros as invalid JSON. It will accept negative zero as an integer token and canonicalize it to 0. Tokens containing a decimal point, exponent, or E are treated as floats rather than integers.\n\nThe parser will reject `+Infinity` and lowercase `infinity` as invalid tokens. It will accept standard JSON whitespace between tokens. String escapes include `\\\"`, `\\\\`, `\\/`, `\\b`, `\\f`, `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t`, and `\\uXXXX`. Invalid escapes and unescaped control characters in strings are parse errors.\n\nThe parser will reject unescaped control characters in strings as parse errors. It will also reject duplicate object keys even when the values are identical. Nesting depth is tracked by incrementing on `{` and `[` and decrementing on `}` and `]`; a depth exceeding 512 triggers an error.\n\nA depth of 512 is permitted; only 513 or more triggers an error. The events directory may be empty, yielding an empty list. Files inside it are sorted by name and parsed as individual JSON values; non-file entries are skipped. A JSON array in a file counts as one element, but it is not treated as a record object and is ignored during binding. If parent.json exists, it must parse successfully.\n\nIf parent.json exists but parent_contract is null, the program ignores it. If parent_contract is present and parent.json is missing, it reports parent_not_supplied. The nenrin directory is walked once per file, sorted by filename; any invalid JSON in a file causes an error.\n\nThe program must error if view.json or contract.json is missing from the vector directory. The grant subset rule is being re-examined: an action is skipped when it is in prohibited_actions, or when it is absent from both authorized_actions and any conditional entry. A violation occurs if no conditional entry matches the action, or if a matching entry requires a different reason.\n\nA child contract that unconditionally authorizes an action violates the grant subset rule if the parent requires it conditionally with a specific reason. The max_hops field must be an integer less than or equal to p minus one; equal to p counts as above p minus one. Boolean true is not an integer.\n\nA child contract with max_hops set to true violates the grant subset rule if the parent has an integer max_hops. The expiry field may equal the parent's expiry; only a value strictly above it is invalid. Depth comparisons allow equality. If the parent lacks max_target_bits, the child's target is not checked.\n\nRule 13 requires the child's approval_policy.allow_unscoped to be true when the parent's is absent or false. Rule 14 compares approver sets using canonical bytes; a missing child approvers set differs from a well-formed parent set and causes a violation. Core v0 does not support well-formed approvers in the child.\n\nThe door function returns True for well-formed approvers instead of refuse. Early out-of-scope checks on the child contract now include spine, convergence, and admission requirements. A child with well-formed approvers or any of these fields present is rejected before core v0 processing begins.\n\nThe limits requirement is out of scope when present and non-null. A missing or non-object requirements field does not trigger spine checks, so a child contract without requirements is valid. The implementation will be written carefully to produce a working program.\n\nStrict values require every object key to be ASCII printable, excluding control characters and DEL. Strings may contain non-ASCII bytes but must not include control characters or DEL. Every number must be an integer within ±(2^53-1).\n\nBody strictness and anchor strictness require additional checks beyond the value being a strict value. Signature entries must be strict values for strictly signed records. When signing a record, the canonical form excludes the signatures key if it is absent.\n\nStrictly signed records require a valid signatures entry; otherwise they are not strictly signed. A non-strict body without strict signing is set aside with no reason, while a strict body that is not strictly signed and lacks lax verification is rejected. The record SHA-256 for dispositions is computed from the canonical form of the entire record. During binding, identical events are skipped and only one disposition is emitted. Records with the same body but different signatures have different signing bytes.\n\nRecords that differ only in unsigned parts—signatures or anchor—share the same signing bytes. The binding step groups them together, and rule 6.4 collapses each group into a single record. The kept record is the one with the smallest height and record SHA-256 among those that anchor validly.\n\nThe duplicate-dropped disposition is removed. A record that anchors validly may still fall below the checkpoint lower bound; it is kept because it has the smallest height, and rule 7.4 marks it as anchor_below_lower_bound.\n\nDropped records are not examined further. The settlement is underspecified when records fall below the lower bound.\n\nRow 5 of triage keeps a bound record with the same signing bytes if it anchors validly. Non-strict anchors are set aside without reason when a good copy exists.\n\nA record with a non-strict anchor is not kept by the table. The kept record must have a strict anchor, strict body, strictly signed status, and no invalid revocation without policy.\n\nThe table logic requires two passes over bound records. The first pass classifies each record as strictly signed, body strict, anchor strict, and whether it anchors validly. The second pass applies the triage table rules. A record is only considered to anchor validly if the view has already passed verification under rules 1 through 8.\n\nThe anchors_validly check is defined as an object with a non-negative integer height, a matching view header hash, a successful proof reaching the Merkle root, and an accepted batch leg. It does not verify the lower bound or the block hash format beyond equality.\n\nUppercase hex in proofs or anchors fails strictness checks and anchors_validly. The view will be indexed by height to map each header to its hash, bytes, Merkle root, and target. The nBits check and linkage will be verified during view construction.\n\nThe header parser extracts the previous hash, Merkle root, nBits, and block hash from the first 160 hex characters. The target is computed from nBits as a 256-bit big-endian integer. The header hash must be less than or equal to this target and less than or equal to the floor value. An invalid nBits value causes rule 7 to fail, and an invalid floor causes rule 1 to fail.\n\nZero mantissa in max_target_bits is invalid. Both door and view refuse it. If section 4 fails, the view is still checked and both reasons apply. Records are not examined when section 4 fails.\n\nDispositions are produced only during the binding step. The contract_signature_not_strict reason is added in 4.3 even when 4.1 fails. A signature is not strict if it decodes to 64 bytes but fails the strictness checks: S must be less than L, or R must not decode as a valid point, or R must be the identity point, or R must have small order. If base64 does not decode to exactly 64 bytes, this reason is not added. Multiple signatures are checked individually and the reason appears once per violation. A signature that is not a string or cannot be decoded produces no reason from 4.3 because 4.1 already rejects it.\n\nA parent contract is present if its key exists and the value is not null. An empty object still counts as present, but a missing contract_id causes narrowing to fail. The child's own contract_id may differ from the parent's, so the check compares the child's parent_contract.contract_id against the parent's contract_id.\n\nRevision 0.6 clarifies that failing section 4.1 while a parent is supplied should only add the parent_narrowing_failed reason. It must not also add contract_not_verified.\n\nThe verdict will contain only core fields, omitting any informative metadata. The final logic gathers underspecified reasons into a set.\n\nThe final verdict is determined by the collected reasons and deviations. If reasons exist, the verdict is underspecified and deviations are discarded. If deviations exist, the verdict is deviation with a sorted list of codes and a computed status. The evidence-missing check in section 11.2 runs only when no reasons exist and either deviations or accepted executions are present.\n\nThe final verdict is nothing_to_settle when no event set exists and no reasons are collected. Otherwise it is within_grant or deviation. Section 11.2 runs after the preliminary verdict is chosen. If evidence is required and an accepted execution's nenrin_ref is not a 64-character hex string, the verdict becomes deviation.\n\nStatus is undetermined when the verdict is underspecified. Otherwise it is final if every accepted and orphaned height is at most the horizon, or provisional otherwise. The horizon equals tip minus k plus one, where k is the depth. A record at that exact height is final, and one block above it is provisional.\n\nThe bond outcome is determined by the verdict and status. It is undetermined when the verdict is underspecified. It is pending_finality when the status is provisional. It is held for within_grant or nothing_to_settle. It is forfeited for deviation. A missing or null bond yields n/a.\n\nThe bond outcome is undetermined when the bond is a dictionary with a truthy amount field. Otherwise it is n/a. Python treats null, false, 0, 0.0, empty strings, lists, and dictionaries as falsy. It treats non-empty containers, nonzero numbers, true, and NaN as truthy.\n\nA helper function `py_truthy` will replicate Python's truthiness semantics. A well-formed bond amount is an integer from 1 to 2^53-1, excluding booleans; amounts of exactly 2^53-1 are valid. If the bond is malformed, the `bond_malformed` reason already forces an underspecified verdict, so the well-formed path is skipped.\n\nA bond amount of exactly 2^53 is invalid. It fails the strict integer check in rule 11 and the range check in 4.3, so both contract_not_verified and bond_malformed apply. The object is truthy, which makes the bond malformed and the outcome undetermined.\n\nA contract can pass section 4.1 while the bond is malformed. Rule 11 does not check the bond, so a string amount or boolean amount would not fail 4.1. Section 4.3 still adds the bond_malformed reason and forces an underspecified verdict. The event set is still computed, deviations are cleared, and the bond outcome becomes undetermined.\n\nA bond amount of 0 passes rule 11 because zero is a valid integer, but it fails the range check in 4.3 and is falsy in Python, so the outcome is n/a. The delegation deviation applies only when delegated_to strings are not exact matches against allowed values. Conditional actions in the grant are identified by their action field.\n\nAn execution is unauthorized if its action is not in the authorized list and is neither conditional nor prohibited. An empty authorized list makes every non-prohibited, non-conditional action unauthorized; this is not an error. If no executions exist, the verdict is nothing_to_settle.\n\nProhibited actions are checked first. After_expiry applies to every string action of an execution with height above expiry_height, but it does not stop other checks. Both after_expiry and prohibited or unauthorized can apply to a single action.\n\nRevoked executions produce only revoked deviations. Expiry and other checks are skipped for them.\n\nEach deviation code appears at most once in the output set. The walk index maps every object by its record_sha256, skipping non-objects and non-strict entries; identical objects share a single index entry.\n\nA non-strict walk whose SHA matches an execution's nenrin_ref triggers the walk_not_strict deviation. Because different content hashes to a different value, this cannot also match a strict walk. If canonical serialization fails for any object, its SHA is recorded as excluded.\n\nThe strictness check applies only the first paragraph of section 6.0, excluding record-specific body checks. The cross-check runs on executions kept by 6.0 that survive rejections from 6.4 and 6.6, including orphaned, anchor_below, and accepted records.\n\nThe cross-check runs after anchors and only when the process has not stopped early. If it adds a deviation but no underspecified reason exists, the verdict becomes deviation. Orphaned executions with invalid nenrin references trigger deviation without adding an underspecified reason. The walk_not_strict deviation remains in the output set.\n\nExecutions set aside by 6.0, rejected in 6.6, and duplicate drops are excluded from later checks. Only records surviving collapse after 6.4 are evaluated. Records with schemas other than the three allowed are silently dropped without a disposition. An unbound disposition applies when a reference lacks contract_sha256 or has null contract_sha256 while its contract_id equals the current cid.\n\nA record is silently dropped when its contract_ref is missing or not an object. The resulting empty reference lacks contract_sha256 and a matching contract_id, so it is never triaged.\n\nThe reference disposition order is: absent or null SHA with matching contract_id yields unbound; absent or null SHA otherwise yields not ours; invalid SHA hex yields inconsistent; mismatched SHA yields foreign; mismatched contract_id yields inconsistent; present payload_digest that does not match yields inconsistent; all remaining cases yield bound. A null or missing payload_digest in the reference is treated as a mismatch.\n\nForeign records are never triaged, even if they are signed. Binding depends solely on contract_sha256; a record under earlier terms with the same contract_id is foreign and excluded from triage. The contract_id comparison is exact, not normalized. If the contract fails section 4.1 because its contract_id is not 32 hex, the binding still proceeds using that identifier.\n\ncontract_id is fetched with `.get` so missing values do not raise errors. The contract SHA is always computed when the object exists. The payload digest is taken from the task object only if it is an object and the key is present; otherwise it is absent. Step 4 of section 7.6 requires the batch leg to parse as strict JSON, so a NaN in the batch causes that leg to be refused.\n\nThe phrase \"strict JSON (2.2)\" refers to section 2.2's reading rules, which treat NaN as a number. The batch leg therefore parses successfully even when it contains NaN.\n\nThe batch leg is parsed with 2.2 reading rules, so NaN is treated as a number and does not trigger a parse error. Schema validation then proceeds normally; if the records list is otherwise valid, the batch may still pass. The test vector likely expects a specific outcome for this case.\n\nDuplicate keys in the batch leg are treated as a parse error under 2.2 rules. The batch is refused with batch_not_json instead of aborting settlement.\n\nA batch leg containing an integer token longer than 4300 digits raises a reader error under section 2.2. The specification requires no settlement in that case, so the parser will abort settlement if it encounters such a token.\n\nThe parser will be parameterized so duplicate keys in a batch leg return batch_not_json rather than aborting settlement. A parsed batch that is not an object may be rejected as batch_schema_unknown.\n\nThe batch leg parser will map specific failures to exact deviation codes. Decode errors and parse failures yield batch_not_json. A non-object batch yields batch_schema_unknown. A schema outside the three allowed values, a records list that is not a list of objects, or a count mismatch yields batch_schema_unknown or batch_count_mismatch. A SHA that is not listed in the batch yields record_not_listed_in_batch. A SHA listed twice yields record_listed_twice. Kind issues and SHA encoding problems map to their respective deviations. The handling for a missing schema key is still being determined.\n\nThe batch schema is strictly nenrin-agreement-batch-v1. Step 8 internal codes are purely informational; the core output always uses anchor_batch_leg_refused for batch-leg refusals, including hexlify_not_leading and batch_leg_malformed. The proof leg is evaluated before the batch leg: if proof validation fails, the result is anchor_proof_invalid and the batch rule is skipped.\n\nThe batch rule accepts proofs that lack hexlify, provided they still reach the merkle root. The example proof uses append/prepend operations rather than hexlify, so it satisfies this condition. The full event proof and walk data are not available for direct tracing.\n\nI will implement the program and run a quick internal test using an RFC Ed25519 vector to verify the implementation. I will also validate canonical serialization and a minimal settlement.\n\nRe-examining section 6.0 shows that a duplicate signing bytes record only counts as kept if it is bound, passed triage, and anchors validly. A same-signing-bytes record that failed strictness or body checks does not satisfy this condition. In the circular case where two records share signing bytes but both fail strictness, neither is kept by the triage table and both anchor with reason anchor_not_strict.\n\nWhen a record has a strict anchor that anchors validly and is kept, a duplicate with a non-strict anchor is set aside without reason. The genuine record settles normally, and no underspecified reason appears. This matches the third-party changes-anchor scenario. If the non-strict copy is the only one, it is discarded as row 6 with an underspecified anchor_not_strict reason.\n\nSet aside records are never examined again. They are excluded from collapse, size limits, conformance checks, and NENRIN evaluation. The 10000-record size limit does not count them. Section 9.2 assigns disposition anchor_not_strict to failing closed records.\n\nRow 4 records are set aside with disposition record_not_strict and a reason, yet they never enter the event set. This makes the entire settlement underspecified while other records continue processing normally. Row 1 records carry disposition record_not_strict but no reason, so they also do not enter the event set.\n\nThe signature check must come after the body strictness check. A record with a non-strict body and a lax signature is classified as row 1, set aside with disposition record_not_strict, rather than signature_not_strict. A strictly signed record requires its signature entry to be a strict value; any float or other non-strict token makes it not strictly signed.\n\nA signature that verifies but is not strictly signed yields disposition signature_not_strict. A strict signature entry containing extra non-strict fields also yields signature_not_strict.\n\nWitness signatures require role \"witness\" and a witness_name matching a grant witness. The key is drawn from that witness. If the grant has no witnesses, witness signatures cannot verify. Triage assumes party keys are available because section 4.1 must pass first.\n\nSection 4.1 guarantees that every party key is a valid 32-byte point, because an invalid point would cause signature verification to return unusable and fail the check. Section 4.2 does not validate points either, so a witness key could be 32 bytes yet still not be a