payload_digest. Values must be integers, booleans, or strings. single_use is canonicalized as true or false. The approval valid_until_height must be an integer. Ed25519 in pure Python is sufficient for a few signatures. Point multiplication is unnecessary for 359 headers.\n\nPure Python Ed25519 verification will use extended coordinates and the binary method. Each 256-bit scalar multiplication takes roughly 10–50 ms, so two per signature verification plus the L-P check total about 100–150 ms. This is acceptable for test vectors. Precomputing base point multiples is skipped for clarity.\n\nPoint equality in projective coordinates will use cross-multiplication to avoid division. The identity point is identified when X equals zero and Y equals Z. Edwards point decompression follows RFC 8032 section 5.1.3, extracting the sign bit from the high byte of the 32-byte input and computing x from y via u = y² − 1 and v = dy² + 1.\n\nThe Edwards point decompression helper now includes the standard recovery formula: compute x from u and v using modular exponentiation, test v·x² − u, switch to the imaginary unit if needed, and flip x’s parity to match the sign bit. It also rejects non-canonical cases where x is zero but the sign bit is set.\n\nThe Edwards point decompression helper now rejects non-canonical points where x is zero but the sign bit is set. The imaginary unit I is computed as pow(2, (p-1)//4, p). After decoding a compressed point, the code verifies it lies on the curve. The base point B is defined with y = 4·inv(5) mod p.\n\nThe base point B is hardcoded with its x and y coordinates. The signature verification hash is SHA-512 of the nonce, public key, and message digest. The scalar k is derived from that digest modulo the curve order L. Verification checks that [S]B equals R plus [k]A. If S is greater than or equal to L, or if R fails decompression, the signature is rejected. Both cofactored and non-cofactored variants are accepted.\n\nThe author is weighing whether to implement the cofactorless Ed25519 equation first, or to add a cofactored fallback for OpenSSL-style lax compatibility. They recall that OpenSSL 3.0's Ed25519 verification checks that S is less than L, and are reviewing how OpenSSL's `ossl_ed25519_verify` handles this.\n\nOpenSSL’s Ed25519 verification may not check that S is less than L in older implementations. The specification explicitly includes the lax case so that a signature which verifies but is not strictly signed yields disposition signature_not_strict. The test vectors synth_signature_s_plus_l and synth_strict_signature_beside_malleated cover this behavior. If the implementation rejects S >= L, a record containing only an S+L signature would be rejected rather than marked as signature_not_strict.\n\nA contract containing only an S+L signature is refused as contract_not_verified and contract_signature_not_strict. A record with a strict signature and a malleated extra entry settles normally because the extra entry is ignored. For contracts, any extra bad signature prevents settlement even when a valid signature exists. If a malleated signature verifies under lax OpenSSL rules but fails strictness, the contract passes step 4.1 yet remains underspecified for contract_signature_not_strict.\n\nThe vector name \"synth_strict_signature_beside_malleated\" implies both a strict signature and a malleated signature are present. Section 13 notes that this test was added after the strict-signature requirement was introduced, replacing an earlier OpenSSL-only acceptance of malleated signatures.\n\nThe `verify()` function is not a strict RFC check. It may return true for lax signatures that are not strictly valid. The specification table indicates that 3.4 can accept non-strict signatures, so the implementation must distinguish between strict and lax verification.\n\nThe `verify()` function performs standard RFC 8032 Ed25519 verification over the 64-byte signature. A signature is strict only when S is less than L and R passes the public-key test. Strict verification therefore applies an additional filter on top of the base check. The reference implementation uses the Python cryptography package backed by OpenSSL, and NOTES.md N9 documents how different libraries handle adversarial signatures.\n\nThe reference implementation uses OpenSSL, which may diverge from strict RFC 8032. The test vectors were generated by that same reference, so the implementation must match OpenSSL’s actual behavior for lax signatures. OpenSSL 3.5 or similar is expected in the cryptography 50.0.1 stack.\n\nOpenSSL 3.1 or a 2023 security fix likely began rejecting non-canonical Ed25519 signatures where S is greater than or equal to L. Modern OpenSSL and the cryptography library almost certainly enforce this check by 2026. If both implementations reject S >= L, the synth_signature_s_plus_l vector would fail verification rather than produce signature_not_strict.\n\nThe first row of Section 13’s test table has no vector for contracts, confirming that an extra bad signature from a party refuses the entire contract even when that party also signed correctly. This suggests synth_strict_signature_beside_malleated is likely a record case where the malleated extra signature is ignored, or it may instead make the approval body non-strict if the malleated entry sits in the approval signature field.\n\nThe implementation will follow RFC 8032 strictly and reject signatures where S is out of range. This matches the spec text and modern OpenSSL. A malleated signature beside a good one in an approval body makes the body non-strict.\n\nRFC 8032 section 5.1.7 explicitly requires that S be decoded as an integer in the range 0 <= s < L, and rejects the signature if S is out of range. This confirms that accepting non-canonical signatures would violate the specification.\n\nThe implementation will use the cofactorless group equation [S]B = R + [k]A' from RFC 8032, rejecting any signature where S is not strictly less than L.\n\nThe RFC treats the cofactored equation as the primary verification check, while the cofactorless alternative is permitted but not required. Implementing only the cofactorless check could reject signatures that the standard check accepts, though strict signatures in the prime subgroup satisfy both.\n\nThe implementation will use the cofactored group equation as the primary RFC 8032 check, rejecting signatures where S is not strictly less than L. This may accept some signatures that a cofactorless check would reject; those edge cases would then be classified as signature_not_strict if the public-key test fails.\n\nThe author is uncertain whether OpenSSL rejects S >= L as early as version 1.1.1, and whether the cryptography library follows that behavior. They will implement a verify function that matches OpenSSL's lax acceptance for malleated signatures while still enforcing strictness on valid ones.\n\nThe author rejected a lax OpenSSL-style fallback that would accept malleated signatures where S is greater than or equal to L. The implementation will strictly follow RFC 8032 and return false for any signature with S out of range. This keeps the strictness check and the triage table consistent.\n\nThe author will strictly follow the specification and reject any signature where S is greater than or equal to L. If test vectors disagree with this behavior, the user can report it.\n\nApproval body strictness is defined independently of verification. A 64-byte sig_b64 that decodes to a non-strict signature—such as one with S equal to L—automatically makes the body non-strict. This does not depend on OpenSSL laxness.\n\nThe `synth_approval_lax_signature_beside_good_sig_b64` vector pairs a non-strict signature with a strict `sig_b64`. The body is non-strict because each key is checked independently. This matches the clarification that a strict `sig_b64` does not excuse a non-strict signature. The `synth_signature_s_plus_l` vector concerns the party Ed25519 signature itself,