 parent-narrowing check. If the child fails, narrowing fails and a separate standalone 4.1 check adds contract_not_verified. Calling door() twice is safe because it has no side effects.\n\nThe author is drafting the `settleable_terms` helper to implement 4.2 checks precisely. It returns a reasons list and only runs when `door()` succeeds. The function begins by extracting the grant dictionary and verifying that `authorized_actions` is a list.\n\nThe `settleable_terms` helper now collects action names from `prohibited_actions` and conditional entries, validates each name against the `[a-z][a-z0-9_.:-]{0,63}` regex, and checks that the witness list does not exceed 32 entries.\n\nThe `settleable_terms` helper now checks for duplicate witness keys and duplicate principal or contractor names. It also validates the `same_height` tie policy, rejecting any value other than `\"conservative\"`. The revocation field is being inspected next.\n\nThe `settleable_terms` helper now validates revocation policies. It requires `effective_at` to be either `\"anchor\"` or `\"delivery_ack\"`, and rejects any other value as non-recomputable. When the effective time is `\"delivery_ack\"`, it checks that `ack_window` is a positive integer. It also verifies that the finality policy contains a depth of at least one.\n\nThe `settleable_terms` helper now validates `expiry_height` as a non-negative integer. It also checks the `approval_policy` dictionary for an `allow_unscoped` key and rejects truthy values, skipping the check when the policy is absent.\n\nThe author clarified the `key_conflict` rule: it triggers when two witnesses share a name, or when any two of the principal key, contractor key, and witness keys decode to the same 32 bytes. Witness names are compared exactly and case-sensitively. Party keys must be base64-encoded Ed25519 public keys.\n\nThe author is now implementing 4.1 signature verification. Rule 15 requires every signature to verify, not merely one per party, and any extra failing signature refuses the contract. An empty signatures list fails rule 16. A domain that fails normalization to a party is refused. The `normalize_domain` helper strips whitespace, lowercases the string, and trims trailing dots.\n\nThe `normalize_domain` helper now rejects domains containing `/`, `@`, `:`, or any whitespace character. It splits the string into labels and validates each label against the standard domain name regex.\n\nThe author realized that the label regex already rejects `/`, `@`, `:`, and spaces, so the explicit character checks in `normalize_domain` are redundant. The helper will instead rely on the label regex, enforce at least two labels, strip whitespace, lowercase, remove trailing dots, and reject empty results.\n\nThe author confirmed that `str.strip()` removes leading tabs and vertical tabs, satisfying the whitespace stripping requirement. They also drafted an `is_https_url` helper that requires a host after `https://` and rejects bare `https://` without a host.\n\nThe author switched to `urllib.parse.urlparse` for HTTPS URL validation, requiring a non-empty host. They noted that `urlparse` lowercases the scheme, which may be overly lenient.\n\nThe author will require the exact case-sensitive prefix `https://` and verify that `urlparse` returns a non-empty host. They are also compiling a list of regular expressions to detect established patterns in text, including phrases like \"performed,\" \"delivered,\" \"payment made,\" and words such as \"contract,\" \"binding,\" and \"escrow.\"\n\nThe established-pattern regex list is finalized with `certified`. Case-insensitive matching handles phrases like \"Money Moved\". Substring checks for \"legal\" and \"deviat\" correctly match longer words such as \"illegal\" and \"deviation\".\n\nRule 7 requires `payload_digest` to be a 64-character lowercase hex string when present. `task.purpose` must be a non-empty string. The parties array must contain exactly two objects, and extra keys in each party object are permitted.\n\nEach party must have role, domain, key_url, and public_key. The public_key is canonical base64 for 32 bytes. Both roles must appear exactly once. The grant object keys are restricted to authorized_actions, prohibited_actions, conditional, and delegation.\n\nThe full set of allowed grant keys is now defined, including data_access, max_hops, privacy, revocation, finality, witnesses, expiry_height, ordering, approval_policy, and limits. authorized_actions and prohibited_actions must both be lists of non-empty strings with no overlap. A null value for either list is refused because rule 9 requires them to be lists.\n\nConditional actions must be unique strings. Each entry object must contain a non-empty string action, and the requires field must be a string when present. Witness names are validated as non-empty strings. The author is ready to write the implementation and will test it with a minimal synthetic case afterward.\n\nThe author will validate the implementation against the RFC 8032 Ed25519 test vector, parse the example contract, and verify its signatures. The expected contract SHA-256 digest is `d7118f285250241513db1ce244a7ec4b4fbfd9160123ba466abdfad727b70f81`. The contract in the prompt is pretty-printed JSON.\n\nThe author will embed a clean Ed25519 module directly in the file. Scalar multiplication will use double-and-add from MSB, with point validation results cached by key bytes to avoid redundant prime-order checks.\n\nThe author is implementing the Ed25519 scalar multiplication loop using LSB-first bit scanning. They will use extended coordinates for point addition and verify that identity elements behave correctly. The implementation will be tested against the RFC 8032 zero-length message test vector.\n\nThe author will test the Ed25519 implementation against the RFC 8032 zero-length message test vector. They plan to verify small-order public keys are rejected, including the identity point.\n\nThe author will validate the Ed25519 scalar multiplication by checking that `[L]B` equals the identity point and `[2]B` equals `B+B`. They also plan to implement a bytes-based JSON parser for batch legs that can optionally record string offsets. The implementation is large, so they will write it systematically.\n\nThe author is clarifying batch JSON strictness. NaN values parse successfully as numbers, so the batch is not refused for failing strict JSON parsing. The refusal likely occurs during schema validation instead. If NaN appears in an unused field, the batch may still pass.\n\nThe author confirmed that NaN in batch bytes does not abort settlement; it is handled by the batch rule. Both `batch_not_json` and `batch_schema_unknown` outcomes map to `anchor_batch_leg_refused`. The parser will continue to schema validation after successfully parsing a JSON object containing NaN.\n\nThe author decided to require 6.0 strict JSON parsing for batch legs. A parsed batch that fails strictness or schema validation will be refused, ensuring NaN values are rejected without affecting core output.\n\nThe author reconsidered requiring 6.0 strict JSON for batch legs. The specification only mandates \"strict JSON (2.2)\", so a batch containing a float in an extra field might be valid under the current standard. They are now weighing whether to accept such batches or refuse them, since NaN values could otherwise pass schema checks without triggering a refusal.\n\nThe author decided not to specially refuse NaN values in batch legs beyond normal schema checks. The specification note clarifies that NaN parses as a number without causing a JSON syntax error, so the batch may still fail schema validation if NaN appears in a restricted field.\n\nThe author clarified batch validation rules. A `count` field that is a float but numerically equal to the record length will be rejected as a type mismatch. Each record entry must be an object; otherwise the batch fails schema validation. The SHA comparison requires an exact string match, and if no record matches the expected digest the result is `record_not_listed_in_batch`.\n\nStep 8 only applies when exactly one record matches the SHA. The SHA must be a string, and its raw encoding is checked. For typed batches, the record kind maps to \"execution\" for schema \"a2a-execution-v0\"; any other kind yields `record_kind_mismatch`. A present but non-matching `schema` field yields `record_schema_mismatch`, including null values. The author also noted a hexlify position issue.\n\nThe author is implementing the hexlify proof rule. A hexlify operation must be the first element of the proof list; if it appears elsewhere or multiple times, the batch is refused. If no hexlify exists, the rule is skipped. After confirming a leading hexlify, the code walks the remaining operations to collect prep and app steps until it reaches the sha256 step.\n\nThe proof walker validates each operation as a dictionary. It collects hex append and prepend operands into byte arrays, rejects unknown operations, and requires a sha256 step to terminate the walk.\n\nThe author finalized the proof reconstruction. The body digest is encoded as lowercase ASCII hex, reversed prep bytes are joined first, then appended bytes, and the resulting batch is hashed.\n\nPrepend operands are reversed because each prepend inserts at the front, so the last one becomes the leading prefix. Each hex operand must be a lowercase hex string of at most 4096 bytes; empty strings are allowed.\n\nThe author is now drafting the `anchors_validly` proof walker. It validates that the proof is a non-empty list of at most 512 operations, initializes the current state to the body digest bytes, and begins iterating over each operation.\n\nThe proof walker now implements the sha256, hexlify, append, and prepend operations. Hex operands are validated as lowercase hex strings of at most 4096 bytes. Each operation updates the running state, and the walker rejects any state exceeding 65536 bytes. The function returns true if the final state matches the merkle root.\n\nThe author noted that hexlify works on any current state length. The walker must verify the final state exactly equals the 32-byte merkle root.\n\nFor anchor validation, a record is incomplete if its anchor lacks a height or if block_hash is not a 64-character lowercase hex string. The height extraction returns None when the anchor is missing or invalid. The block_hash check runs even when height exists, and the lower bound check is skipped entirely when no height is present.\n\nThe author is now considering the out-of-scope check. It should run after confirming the record is an object, and they are deciding whether to output anything when a record is out of scope.\n\nThe author decided to emit an error and exit with code 2 when a record is out of scope, rather than attempting to compute on it. They confirmed the baseline contract contains no `spine` requirement, and reviewed the baseline grant fields: no expiry_height, ordering, approval_policy, or limits; finality includes depth and max_target_bits; revocation uses an effective_at anchor; witnesses, conditional, and delegation allowed are empty.\n\nThe baseline contract satisfies rule 4.2: finality depth is 6, revocation uses an anchor, there is no tie policy, no unscoped requirements, action names match the pattern, witnesses are zero, and there is no key conflict.\n\nFor evidence, the execution contains a 64-character hex nenrin_ref, so no evidence_missing deviation applies if the record is accepted. The cross-check for supplied nenrin records only runs when the list is non-empty; otherwise rule 11.1 is skipped. Rule 11.2 still validates the reference format on accepted executions, and the hex string passes.\n\nThe author will test contract SHA and signature verification after writing the code. Contract signing bytes exclude the signatures key. Canonical JSON ignores whitespace, so reformatted input is acceptable. Signature verification will use the principal key and signature from the example.\n\nThe author plans to use `json.dumps` with `ensure_ascii=True`, `sort_keys=True`, and custom separators to match the specification's field order. Disposition values will be strings, and `contract_sha256` will be null when absent. A trailing newline will be added via `print`. Heights are small enough to avoid JSON number precision issues.\n\nThe author will sort disposition keys for stable output. The event set must be ordered by height, then by SHA as lowercase hex strings. Record SHA-256 is the lowercase hex digest of the canonical record bytes.\n\nThe author defined `JInt` and `JFloat` wrapper classes to preserve exact JSON number semantics during parsing. Anchor heights will be converted to Python integers early for validation.\n\nThe author defined `is_int` to accept `JInt` values while rejecting booleans, and `is_int_ge` with an optional lower bound. They set `MAX_SAFE = 2**53 - 1` as the safe integer limit. A recursive `is_strict` function will reject `JFloat` values and enforce the safe-range constraint on `JInt`. They also planned helper functions `walk_numbers` and `walk_keys` for rule 11.\n\nThe `is_strict` function now rejects strings containing ASCII control characters or the DEL byte. It recursively validates lists and dictionaries, ensuring all keys are valid ASCII strings without control characters.\n\nThe author decided to enforce the 512-level depth limit inside `is_strict` by counting container nesting depth. A root object is at depth 0, and nested containers increment the count; any value deeper than 512 levels is rejected as non-strict.\n\nThe author will count container nesting depth by bracket pairs, matching the parser's style. ASCII keys must be printable ASCII without control characters or DEL. Strings may contain non-ASCII bytes but must not contain control characters or DEL. A `body_not_strict` helper is being drafted to validate record bodies while excluding `signatures` and `anchor`.\n\nThe `body_not_strict` helper now rejects records with invalid `nenrin_ref`, `contract_sha256`, or `payload_digest` hex values. It also checks that `performed_actions` is a list of strings for the `a2a-execution-v0` schema.\n\nThe `body_not_strict` helper now also rejects records with invalid `delegated_to` lists or `approvals` objects. It validates that approval entries contain valid base64 signatures.\n\nThe author confirmed that `body_not_strict` catches invalid `nenrin_ref` hex values even when the string is otherwise strict. They also began drafting `anchor_not_strict`, which rejects anchors that are not strict or contain an invalid 64-character `block_hash`.\n\nThe `anchor_not_strict` helper now validates proof operations. It rejects any operation whose `hex` field is not a lowercase hex string. If the anchor is not a dictionary, it is treated as strict as a value unless `is_strict` rejects it.\n\nThe author realized that a string anchor is technically strict as a value, so `anchor_not_strict` returns false for it. The record is still incomplete because the anchor is not an object and lacks a height.\n\nThe author drafted `sig_not_strict_b64` to reject base64 signatures that do not decode to 64 bytes. They also defined `signature_is_strict` to validate Ed25519 signatures by checking the scalar S against the curve order L, decoding the point R, rejecting identity points, and verifying R lies in the prime subgroup.\n\nThe author drafted the `verify` function. It canonicalizes the public key and signature, rejects unusable inputs such as invalid points or identity keys, decodes the signature point R and scalar S, and returns 'false' when S is not strictly less than the curve order L.\n\nThe author completed the `verify` function by computing the challenge scalar `k` from the signature prefix, public key, and message. It checks the primary Ed25519 equation `[S]B == R + [k]A`. They are considering whether to accept a cofactored verification when the cofactorless check fails, though this would indicate the point is not in the prime subgroup.\n\nThe author decided to accept a signature if either the cofactorless or cofactored Ed25519 equation holds. This is a superset of valid signatures, but it ensures the disposition matches any compliant implementation.\n\nThe author abandoned the OR fallback between cofactorless and cofactored Ed25519 checks. They will use only the cofactored equation, which is the standard RFC check. This avoids accepting signatures that OpenSSL might reject. The implementation will multiply both sides of the equation by 8 to clear the cofactor.\n\nThe author will test both cofactorless and cofactored Ed25519 checks against the RFC vector. The prime subgroup test will cache results by compressed point bytes to avoid repeated scalar multiplications.\n\nThe author will cache successful signature verifications per public key to avoid redundant subgroup checks. They will also add a note after the code block about ambiguities in the specification. The complete program must be placed inside a single Python fenced code block.\n\nThe author will parse all files in the events directory, including non-JSON ones. They noted that duplicate validation reasons should be collected in a set to avoid duplicates. They also clarified that signature strictness checks must run even when contract verification fails, and that missing signatures on an object contract trigger a contract_not_verified reason rather than a signature reason.\n\nThe author clarified that binding must occur whenever the contract is an object, even if the door check fails. They also noted that a missing events directory yields an empty list, leaving bound records without dispositions while foreign, inconsistent, and unbound records still receive them. The program will halt before triage when view rules 1–8 fail, or when headers exceed 200,000 after verification.\n\nBinding still runs when view rules 4 or 5 fail, and its classes are reported. A single `chain_view_rejected` reason is used even if multiple rules fail, while `view_not_strict` remains separate. If the view verifies but holds more than 200,000 headers, the disposition is `input_too_large`; otherwise it is `chain_view_rejected`. Rule 9 is checked even when earlier rules fail or the input is too large.\n\nThe author drafted the `check_view` function. It verifies rules 1–8 first, then checks for `input_too_large` if the header count exceeds 200,000. Rule 9 is only evaluated when the view is an object with a headers list. The function returns the reasons list, a boolean flag to stop examination, and the view index.\n\nThe author noted that rule 9 requires every header to be an object with a height and a hex string of exactly 160 lowercase hex characters. They also observed that a negative integer height would satisfy rule 9's \"integer height\" requirement, but rule 4's non-negative constraint would still reject it.\n\nRule 9 height is interpreted as any integer, including negative and large values. A boolean height fails rule 9 but passes rule 4, so only `view_not_strict` is reported. Rule 4 still rejects negative heights.\n\nHuge integers satisfy rule 4's non-negative height requirement but fail rule 9's strictness check, so only `view_not_strict` is reported and examination continues. Rule 9 also requires every string in the view to be strict.\n\nThe author drafted `get_floor` to extract the finality floor from a contract. It reads `grant.finality.max_target_bits`, validates it as an 8-character hex string, converts it to an integer, and passes it to `compact_target`. That helper returns None for invalid input or zero, and the function returns None on any exception.\n\nThe `compact_target` helper decodes the 32-bit target from its compact representation. It returns None for invalid inputs, zero, or non-positive results. Rule 1 requires a valid floor; if missing, the view is rejected as `chain_view_rejected`.\n\nThe author noted that rule 4.1 requires `max_target_bits` to decode to a positive target, using the same helper as the finality floor check. They also drafted validation for `lower_bound`: it must be an object with an integer height and a 64-character lowercase hex hash. The spec does not explicitly require the height to be non-negative, so any integer value is accepted.\n\nThe author added an `is_hex_n` helper to validate strings as exactly n lowercase hex characters. They noted that a negative `lower_bound` height will fail rule 8 because no header exists at that height, so the view is rejected as `chain_view_rejected`. They also began handling optional line feeds in the view headers hex for rules 1–8.\n\nThe author drafted `header_bytes_from_hex` to parse header hex strings, accepting either 160 or 161 characters with an optional trailing newline. They also began implementing linkage checks: the previous hash field of each header must match the computed hash of the preceding header.\n\nThe author implemented the own-hash check by double-SHA256 of the raw header bytes, reversing the digest, and converting it to a hex string. They added the nBits target comparison against the floor and the hash integer, rejecting when the target is missing, above the floor, or the hash exceeds the target. Rule 8 is satisfied if any header matches the lower_bound height and hash; consecutive headers make this unique. They also extracted the merkle root from bytes 36–68.\n\nThe author will build ordered header lists and a height-indexed dictionary. The Tip is the last height. Settlement skips the index if earlier rules fail. They are now drafting the `evaluate` function for section 10. It filters event records into revocations and acknowledgments. It reads the revocation policy from the contract grant.\n\nThe author clarified that event records must be accepted only if the revocation policy is an object; otherwise they are set aside. They also noted that copies of records remain in the input after step 6.4 even if later orphaned or anchor-failed, so acknowledgment names a revocation when its SHA-256 matches either the body digest or the record SHA-256 of such a surviving copy.\n\nDropped records are excluded from the walk. The post-6.4 record list must be passed to the walk, not merely the accepted subset. An acknowledgment naming a kept-but-orphaned copy's record SHA-256 counts toward revocation, because that copy remains in the input after step 6.4; an acknowledgment naming a dropped copy does not.\n\nThe author clarified that an acknowledgment naming a kept-but-orphaned copy's record SHA-256 counts toward revocation, since that copy remains in the input after step 6.4. They also noted that the body digest of an orphaned copy can match the original revocation if signatures are identical and only the anchor differs, because the body digest excludes the anchor.\n\nThe author drafted the acknowledgment matching logic. For each accepted revocation, they collect its body digest and the record SHA-256 of every surviving post-6.4 copy that shares the same signing bytes. This set of identifiers is used to determine which revocations are named by acknowledgments.\n\nThe author drafted the execution height calculation. It takes the minimum of the revocation height plus the acknowledgment window and the heights of accepted acknowledgments that name a revocation. If no acknowledgments name any revocation, the execution height is simply the revocation height plus the window. Execution is revoked when any revocation's execution height is less than or equal to the current execution height.\n\nThe author began drafting a `classify_approval` helper to detect forged approvals. It validates the approval dictionary and checks that `valid_until_height` is a non-negative integer, `nonce` is a 32-character hex string, `single_use` is a boolean, and `contract_sha256` is a 64-character hex string. It then extracts the base64 signature field.\n\nThe `classify_approval` helper canonicalizes the approval fields into a message prefixed with `\"a2a-approval-v2\\n\"`. It verifies the Ed25519 signature against that message. A valid signature yields `\"counted\"` if the contract SHA-256 matches the current contract, or `\"other_terms\"` otherwise. The author also began drafting support for older v1 approval forms.\n\nThe author completed the v1 approval validation. It checks that `valid_until_height` is a non-negative integer, `nonce` is a 32-character hex string, and `single_use` is a boolean. It then extracts the contract ID and payload digest from the task object, defaulting to null if absent. If these fields are valid, it canonicalizes an approval message prefixed with `\"a2a-approval-v1\\n\"` and containing the contract ID, payload digest, action, validity height, and nonce.\n\nThe `classify_approval` helper now completes the v1 approval path. It canonicalizes a message prefixed with `\"a2a-approval-v1\\n\"` and returns `\"label_bound\"` if the Ed25519 signature verifies. It then tries an `\"a2a-approval-v0\\n\"` prefix and returns `\"label_bound\"` on success, or `\"forged\"` otherwise. Missing fields are canonicalized as null, and the action field is used even when absent or non-string. The v0 path accepts any signature that verifies regardless of malformed fields.\n\nThe author confirmed that `verify` returns `unusable` or `false` for invalid signatures. They noted the principal key comes from the parties object and will be base64-decoded. They began drafting conditional approval matching by iterating over the event set in order, skipping non-execution records, classifying each approval, and collecting classified approvals into a list while recording any `forged_approval` deviations.\n\nThe author collected SHA-256 hashes of revoked executions into a set. They flagged any performed action on a revoked record as a deviation. They then began iterating over unrevoked executions to check expiry and validate performed actions.\n\nThe author added expiry checks to the unrevoked execution loop. Actions exceeding the expiry height trigger an `after_expiry` deviation. Prohibited actions trigger a `prohibited` deviation. Conditional actions trigger a `conditional` deviation when approval is missing. Unauthorized actions trigger an `unauthorized` deviation. The loop then processes the `delegated_to` list against the allowed delegation set.\n\nThe author added a `delegation` deviation for any string in `delegated_to` that is not in the allowed set. They also drafted the `approved()` helper: it scans counted approvals in event order, matching the action and checking that the approval record SHA-256 or height precedes the current execution, that the height is within the approval's validity window, and that a single-use nonce has not already been consumed; it marks the nonce as used when applicable.\n\nThe author confirmed that scanning counted approvals in event order is correct because later records have higher or equal height, so the height filter naturally excludes them. They also verified that action equality uses exact string comparison and that the validity-height check compares JInt values against integers.\n\nRevoked executions remain in the event set, so their counted approvals are included in the approval scan and can be consumed by later executions. Nonces on revoked records are not consumed during judgment because those records are skipped in the judge loop. The author also began building the conditional actions set from the grant's `conditional` list.\n\nConditional actions are stored as a dictionary keyed by exact action string. The authorized and prohibited sets are built from the grant. Allowed delegation principals come from the grant's `delegation.allowed` list, defaulting to empty if absent. The expiry height is read from the grant and converted to an integer.\n\nThe author drafted the NENRIN cross-check logic. It iterates over NENRIN records, computes their SHA-256 digests from canonical forms, and separates strict records into an index while collecting non-strict ones into a left-out set. It then begins comparing these against post-6.4 executions to identify which records are kept by rule 6.0 but rejected by rule 6.6.\n\nThe author completed the NENRIN cross-check loop. It extracts each execution's `nenrin_ref`, flags it as `walk_not_strict` if the referenced record is non-strict, and looks up that record in the strict index. If the record exists and its context contains a `contract_sha256` that differs from the current contract SHA-256, it adds an `evidence_names_other_contract` deviation.\n\nThe NENRIN cross-check targets executions that survived section 6.0 and section 6.4, including orphaned and incomplete anchor records. Rejected executions are excluded. Duplicate drops are also excluded. Rule 11.2 is applied separately to accepted records only.\n\nThe author added a rule 11.2 check that requires every accepted execution to carry a valid 64-character hex `nenrin_ref` when the grant demands NENRIN evidence, adding an `evidence_missing` deviation otherwise. They also defined the set of required execution fields for conformance validation.\n\nThe author defined the allowed field sets for revocation and ack records. They compiled a regex to validate action strings. The `conformance_problem` helper checks that each record contains only allowed fields for its schema.\n\nThe `conformance_problem` helper now validates execution records. It checks that `performed_actions` is a list of at most 256 strings. It also verifies that any `outcomes` dictionary keys are drawn from the performed actions list and values are limited to `\"ok\"`, `\"error\"`, `\"timeout\"`, or `\"partial\"`.\n\nThe `conformance_problem` helper now validates every action string in `performed_actions` against the regex. It also checks that each approval dictionary contains a valid action string. The author noted that dictionary keys are always strings in JSON, so outcome keys are inherently valid.\n\nThe author added a `has_contractor_sig` helper that checks contractor Ed25519 signatures against the signing bytes. A record with a conformance problem and a valid contractor signature is marked as a deviation. Otherwise it is rejected. Witness-only records with problems are also rejected.\n\nThe author drafted the `strictly_signed` helper. It checks that a record carries at least one signature, computes the signing bytes, and iterates over each signature entry to match its role against the contract and verify the Ed25519 signature.\n\nThe `strictly_signed` helper now returns both a strict signature flag and a lax flag. It checks that each signature entry is strict and its raw bytes are strict before marking the record as fully signed.\n\nThe `matching_key` function maps execution roles to the contractor key, witness roles to the exact witness name, revocation roles to the principal key, and ack roles to the contractor key. It returns None for any other combination.\n\nWitness lookup begins by extracting the witness name from the signature entry and scanning the witnesses list.\n\nThe witness lookup now returns the Ed25519 public key when a matching witness name is found. The author noted that well-formed grants are assumed, but defensive coding will still be used. They also defined `record_signing_bytes`, which prepends a schema-specific context prefix to the canonical JSON body while omitting `signatures` and `anchor`.\n\nThe author noted that triage row 5 requires a valid view before anchor checks can proceed. They defined `body_digest` to compute the SHA-256 of the canonical record body excluding the `anchor` field, and `record_sha` to hash the full canonical record. If canonicalization fails, the record is set aside as `record_not_strict` without a disposition.\n\nThe author noted that `record_sha` is used for binding. If canonicalization fails, the record is set aside as `record_not_strict` before binding occurs. This is a set-aside reason code rather than an underspecified reason.\n\nThe author clarified that a record set aside as `record_not_strict` because its SHA-256 cannot be computed receives no disposition and is counted in the informative refused tally. This differs from row 4, which explicitly adds the underspecified reason to the settlement.\n\nThe author decided not to add the underspecified reason for undigestable records, since they have no disposition and are only counted in the informative refused tally. They also began implementing group collapse by signing bytes among kept records.\n\nThe group collapse logic now keeps one record per signing bytes. If multiple copies share the same SHA-256, only the first is retained. Otherwise valid copies are sorted by height and SHA-256, the earliest is kept as the winner, and all other duplicates in the group are marked `duplicate_dropped`. The author noted that because section 6.1 already merged identical records, every copy within a group should have a distinct SHA-256.\n\nThe author confirmed that duplicate-dropping logic correctly handles groups with multiple distinct SHA-256 values. They noted that conformance checks and anchor validation will run on the final kept records, and that every record will receive a disposition at the end of processing.\n\nRecords below the lower bound receive a reason and disposition, but are excluded from the event set. Orphaned records are marked orphaned with no reason, and their heights are tracked for status computation. The `compute_status` function compares accepted and orphaned heights against a horizon derived from the tip and depth; if any height exceeds the horizon, the status is provisional, otherwise final. When the verdict is underspecified, the status is undetermined and this step is skipped.\n\nThe author added an `input_too_large` guard that halts processing when more than 10,000 kept records remain after collapse. They noted that bound records failing the 6.2 check carry no disposition, while set-aside records retain their prior dispositions.\n\nThe author confirmed that bound records failing the 6.2 strict-signature check retain their binding dispositions, since only set-aside records lose them. They also verified that an `input_too_large` halt leaves the event set empty and yields an underspecified verdict with undetermined status. They are now checking whether skipping triage when 4.2 fails is consistent with section 9.2.\n\nThe author confirmed that bound records failing section 4.2 have no disposition, while foreign records retain theirs. They began adding parent checks in 4.3, including a strict signature validation for contract signatures.\n\nThe `check_4_3` helper now validates the contract bond amount and checks parent contract narrowing when a parent is supplied.\n\nThe `parent_narrowing_ok` helper returns False when the child door check fails, the parent is not a dictionary, the parent lacks a door, the contract IDs differ, or the child grant is not within the parent grant.\n\nThe author confirmed that `contract_not_verified` is already added by the door check, so both reasons can coexist. They verified that `grant_within` only runs after both doors pass and that it returns False when the child grant is not within the parent grant. They began implementing `grant_within` with a helper to safely extract list values from grant dictionaries.\n\nThe author defined helper functions to safely extract lists and integers from grant dictionaries. They confirmed that JInt correctly excludes booleans for integer checks. They began implementing Rule 1 of `grant_within`, requiring the child's authorized actions to be a subset of the parent's.\n\nThe author completed Rule 2 of `grant_within`, requiring the child's prohibited actions to be a subset of the parent's. They added Rule 3, requiring the child's data access list to be a subset of the parent's. Rule 4 enforces that the child's `max_hops` is present and does not exceed the parent's value minus one. They began implementing Rule 5, which extracts allowed delegation roles from the grant dictionary.\n\nThe author completed Rule 5 of `grant_within`, requiring the child's allowed delegation roles to be a subset of the parent's. They began implementing Rule 6, which compares conditional actions and builds a mapping from conditional actions to their corresponding authorized actions.\n\nThe author completed Rule 6 of `grant_within`, ensuring conditional actions require matching `requires` values between the child and parent grants. They then began implementing Rule 7.\n\nRule 7 of `grant_within` requires every child conditional action to be either an authorized action or a conditional action in the parent grant. Rule 8 enforces that the child's expiry height is present and does not exceed the parent's. Rule 9 begins comparing revocation effective times between the child and parent grants.\n\nRule 9 of `grant_within` now enforces revocation effective times. It requires the child's `effective_at` to be a dictionary containing that key. Anchor mode must match exactly. Delivery-ack mode allows anchor or delivery-ack; if delivery-ack, the child's `ack_window` must not exceed the parent's.\n\nThe author defined a nested integer helper for Rule 10. It requires the child finality dictionary to be present and checks that the child depth is at least the parent depth. It also begins comparing max_target_bits hex strings.\n\nRule 10 now completes by decoding the child's `max_target_bits` hex string and ensuring it is present, decodes correctly, and does not exceed the parent's value. Rule 11 begins by extracting the parent's Ed25519 witness public keys from its witness list.\n\nRule 11 of `grant_within` now requires the child's Ed25519 witness public keys to be a subset of the parent's. Rule 12 enforces that both grants set `privacy` and `ordering` identically when either is present. The author began implementing Rule 13, which checks an `approval_policy` field.\n\nRule 13 now requires the child's `allow_unscoped` flag to be exactly `True` when the parent has it enabled. Rule 14 begins by defining an `approver_set` helper to extract approvers from a grant's approval policy, and a `canon_entry` helper to canonicalize entries.\n\nRule 14 now compares canonicalized approver sets from the parent and child grants. It returns False if the sets differ, treating empty sets as equal. The author also began implementing a `json_equal` helper that compares JInt values by their underlying numeric value.\n\nThe `json_equal` helper is now complete, covering JFloat NaN comparisons, dictionary key matching, list element equality, and standard Python types. The author considers the core implementation sufficiently finished and begins adding an out-of-scope check for contracts.\n\nThe `carries_out_of_scope` helper now returns True for contracts carrying spine or convergence requirements, admission requirements, grant limits, or a well-formed approval policy with approvers.\n\nThe author defined `approvers_well_formed` to match rule 10 exactly. It returns True for valid approver lists and False otherwise. Door rule 10 now accepts approval policies with well-formed approvers, deferring out-of-scope classification to the caller.\n\nThe `check_approval_policy` helper returns \"ok\" for valid policies, \"refuse\" for malformed ones, and \"carried\" when approvers are well formed. Child settlements carrying out-of-scope approval policies will be rejected before settlement generation.\n\nThe door will return a three-way status: fail, ok, or carried. A carried result means the contract carries out-of-scope data such as approvers, which makes conformance undefined. The author decided to reject these contracts immediately rather than deferring the error to settlement generation.\n\nThe author began implementing `approvers_well_formed`. It validates that the approver list has between one and sixteen entries, collects unique names and keys, and extracts conditional action names from the conditional list to support later checks.\n\nThe `approvers_well_formed` helper now validates each approver entry. It checks that the entry is a dictionary with only the allowed keys. It verifies the name is a non-empty string and the Ed25519 public key decodes to 32 bytes. It also ensures no duplicate names or public keys appear in the list.\n\n`approvers_well_formed` now validates each approver's actions list. It requires a non-empty list with no duplicate strings, and every action must be a non-empty string present in the parent's conditional actions. The author also decided to enforce uniqueness of public keys using decoded 32-byte Ed25519 values.\n\nThe author confirmed that approvers are invalid when the conditional list is absent, because every action must match an entry in that list. They also verified that name, public_key_ed25519_b64, and actions are all required fields in each approver entry.\n\nThe author is now writing the main program. They added a `load_dir_list` helper to read and sort JSON files from a directory. Missing or null fields will be treated as absent or empty lists. A `read_json_file` helper is also being defined.\n\n`read_json_file` now opens and parses UTF-8 JSON files, raising an error if the file is missing. The `main` function reads the directory path from command-line arguments and loads `contract.json`, `view.json`, and the events directory.\n\nThe main function now handles the events, nenrin, and parent.json paths. It loads event files from a directory or treats a missing path as an empty list. It rejects nenrin when it exists but is not a directory. It reads parent.json only if the file is present.\n\nThe main function now catches JSON parse errors, UTF-8 decode failures, and read errors. It calls `settle` with the loaded data and prints the result as JSON to stdout. A small `error` helper prints an error object and exits with code 2.\n\nThe author decided to parse all files first and exit with code 2 on any JSON parse error. A contract that is not an object still receives a special result even if view.json is valid. Invalid events also trigger an immediate exit. They noted the need to verify integer token length limits during batch parsing.\n\nA `FatalParseError` class will be raised for batch parse failures such as oversized integer tokens. The main function will catch this exception and exit with code 2. Duplicate keys in batch parsing will return a batch refusal instead of failing. Deeply nested JSON text exceeding the 512-level limit will also be treated as a batch refusal rather than a fatal error.\n\nDeeply nested JSON will now be treated as a fatal parse error rather than a batch refusal. This prevents malicious batches from aborting settlement generation instead of being refused. The author is refining the parser exception hierarchy to match this stricter behavior.\n\nThe author decided to treat only oversized integer tokens as fatal input parse errors. Duplicate keys and deeply nested JSON will be caught by the batch parser and returned as batch refusals instead of aborting settlement generation.\n\nThe author decided to propagate fatal parse errors from batch parsing as well. Oversized integer tokens and deeply nested JSON in batches will abort settlement generation rather than return a batch refusal. This matches the reference reader's behavior of raising on 4300-digit integers.\n\nThe batch parser will catch all ParseErrors and return \"batch_not_json\" for everything except integer_too_long and nesting_too_deep, which it re-raises to abort settlement. Duplicate keys in batches are treated as non-fatal input errors.\n\nThe author is implementing the JSON number parser. It handles NaN, Infinity, and -Infinity as special float values. Standard float parsing covers regular numbers and scientific notation. Integer tokens longer than 4300 digits are rejected.\n\nThe author is implementing a manual number scanner to avoid Python's default float behavior. It will detect Infinity and NaN tokens directly, then parse standard numeric tokens with optional minus signs, fractions, and exponents.\n\nThe author verified edge cases for the manual number scanner. It rejects lowercase infinity and plus signs as invalid JSON. Leading zeros in integer tokens are also rejected.\n\nThe author confirmed that `01` is rejected as invalid JSON because the parser consumes only `0` and leaves trailing digits. They also verified that `-0` parses as canonical integer `0`. The string scanner implementation has begun.\n\nThe string scanner now handles escape sequences. It maps backslash characters to their literal equivalents and begins parsing Unicode escapes.\n\nThe string scanner now handles Unicode escapes by decoding four-digit hex sequences into code points. It rejects control characters below 0x20 and invalid escape sequences. The author noted that surrogate pairs parsed as separate characters will be written back as individual \\udxxx escapes, diverging from Python's default behavior of combining them.\n\nThe author realized that lone surrogate characters must be combined into their full Unicode code points during parsing, matching Python's default behavior. A `combine_surrogates` helper is being added to merge consecutive high and low surrogates into single characters.\n\nThe author is now designing a byte-oriented batch parser to track string content offsets, since UTF-8 decoding would shift byte positions. They are also considering a simpler approach for step 8: parsing the batch to locate the sha value and verify it equals the digest.\n\nThe author is refining the batch parser to scan raw bytes for string literals and their content offsets. They will locate the exact sha field by matching its decoded value against the digest, ensuring it contains no escape sequences.\n\nThe author abandoned the byte-oriented parser as too messy. They will instead parse the batch as a string and compute UTF-8 byte offsets on demand using `len(text[:str_index].encode('utf-8'))`. This keeps the parser simple while still tracking exact byte positions for validation.\n\nThe parser will record each string's first character byte offset and flag it if any escape sequences were used. Strings without escapes will be validated against their raw UTF-8 content. The author will implement an optional offset tracker in the parser.\n\nThe author decided to write a separate `parse_json_bytes` function that returns parsed values alongside a list of string locations. Each location records the JSON path, byte start, escape flag, and decoded value. This avoids modifying the standard parser with offset tracking. The parser will build paths by passing a prefix through recursive calls.\n\nThe author finalized the batch validation logic. After parsing, the code locates the `sha` string at path `(\"records\", idx, \"sha\")`. It rejects the batch if that string is missing, contains escape sequences, or does not start exactly at `pre_len`. A valid unescaped 64-character hex string at the correct offset passes.\n\nCanonical JSON object keys will be sorted by Unicode code point using Python's built-in `sorted()`. The author is now ready to write the implementation file.\n\nThe contract signing bytes will exclude the \"signatures\" key before canonical serialization. The parser returns Python booleans for JSON true and false tokens, so boolean fields like allow_unscoped and single_use are checked with isinstance.\n\nThe author decided against adding logging or stderr noise, keeping output strictly to JSON. They will call `error()` from within `settle()` when validation fails, and verified that successful execution returns exit code 0.\n\nThe author is now implementing the `bond_outcome` function. It checks that the contract contains a valid bond dictionary with a safe integer amount. The outcome depends on the verdict and status: \"undetermined\" for underspecified, \"pending_finality\" for provisional status, and specific outcomes for within_grant or nothing_to_settle.\n\nThe `bond_outcome` function now returns \"held\" for within_grant and nothing_to_settle verdicts, and \"forfeited\" for deviation. A malformed bond object with a truthy amount returns \"undetermined\", while a missing amount key returns \"n/a\". A `py_truthy` helper is being defined to test falsiness.\n\nThe `py_truthy` helper is now fully implemented. It returns `False` for `None`, `False`, empty strings, empty lists, and empty dictionaries. Numeric truthiness depends on the underlying value: `JInt` is truthy if its integer is non-zero, while `JFloat` is truthy unless it is exactly `0.0` or `-0.0`; NaN is treated as truthy to match Python semantics.\n\nThe author confirmed that the `bond_outcome` function checks the verdict before the status, so an underspecified verdict takes precedence. They are now beginning to implement a `bind_and_classify` helper for binding dispositions.\n\nThe `bind_and_classify` helper is being implemented. It extracts the contract ID and task payload digest, then iterates over events to validate schemas and compute record SHA-256 digests.\n\nThe `bind_and_classify` helper now skips duplicate SHA-256 digests. It extracts the contract reference and checks whether the event belongs to this contract by matching its ID. Events with a null or absent reference SHA-256 are marked as \"unbound\" when the ID matches. Otherwise they are ignored. The code then begins validating that the reference SHA-256 is a 64-character hex string.\n\nThe `bind_and_classify` helper now marks events as \"inconsistent\" when the reference SHA-256 is malformed, \"foreign\" when it differs from the contract digest, or \"inconsistent\" when the reference contract ID or payload digest mismatches. Valid matches are appended to the bound list. A missing `contract_id` key in the reference is treated as inconsistent because `None != cid`.\n\nThe author noted an edge case where both the contract and reference lack a contract ID; this yields None == None, so the event is treated as unbound if its SHA-256 is absent. They confirmed that payload_digest presence is determined by whether the task object contains a non-null string value. Events with undigestable records will be skipped entirely rather than assigned a disposition.\n\nThe author is now implementing Ed25519 verification using ref10-style formulas. They defined the curve parameters P, L, D, and I, and began computing the base point coordinates. A fast subgroup check cache will be included. The implementation will be written and iterated against test failures.\n\nAffine coordinates will be used for Ed25519 point addition to avoid bugs. The standard twisted Edwards formulas are being adapted for a = -1, with identity at (0, 1) and negation at (-x, y).\n\nThe author finalized the `ed_add` function using the standard twisted Edwards addition formulas for a = -1. They noted that inversion of zero should not occur on valid points, and that scalar multiplication with affine coordinates will be slower due to repeated modular inversions.\n\nScalar multiplication is estimated at roughly fifteen milliseconds per signature, which is acceptable. The author will prioritize correctness over optimization and only add a window method if tests prove too slow. A potential zero denominator in the addition formula was noted but dismissed as impossible for valid curve points. Approval v2 canonical serialization will use an object containing exactly the required fields.\n\nThe author confirmed that extra fields in the approval are excluded from the signed bytes. They verified that a v2 approval with a matching contract SHA-256 is counted as valid. The deviation code list will be sorted in the output. They also drafted the `event_set` logic to build the accepted events array, mapping each record's schema to a kind label and sorting by height and SHA-256.\n\nA small `Rec` class will hold each raw record, its SHA-256 digest, schema, and height. The author is ready to write the implementation and run tests. They also noted three ambiguities to document: rejecting OpenSSL's lax S>=L signature check in favor of RFC 8032; that anchor proof failures do not empty the event set; and that bound records receive no triage dispositions when view rules fail.\n\nThe author noted that out-of-scope returns error, and that batch NaN values are parsed but not specially rejected beyond schema rules. They clarified that when a child fails view rule 4.1 and its parent contract is set, both contract_not_verified and parent_narrowing_failed apply. Binding dispositions still run even if view rules fail, and the contract SHA-256 is still computed regardless. If view rules fail, chain_view_rejected is the only view reason plus possibly view_not_strict. If the grant is missing, door fails and view rule 1 fails, producing two reasons without record examination.\n\nA `party_key` helper will extract Ed25519 public keys from the contract's parties list by role. A witness map will be precomputed. Canonical bytes caching is unnecessary. Hex digests are lowercase.\n\nThe author confirmed that SHA-256 digests are lowercase hex. They noted that an acknowledgment's `revocation_sha256` is not strictly validated for format, so an uppercase value would still be accepted but would fail to match the lowercase body digest. They also observed that the extra-field check does not require `revocation_sha256` to be present.\n\nAn `is_list_of_str` helper will validate that `performed_actions` is a list of strings, allowing empty lists. Execution conformance requires this field to be present. Outcomes keys must not appear in `performed_actions`. The author is ready to write the implementation file.\n\nThe author will embed a test in the shell after writing `settle.py`. They verified the canonical SHA-256 against the known contract hash, so Ed25519 signature verification can be tested once canonical serialization is correct. Ready to code.\n\nThe author will verify canonical base64 signatures against the example. They confirmed the parser handles JSON key order, ASCII and UTF-8 text, and Unicode escapes identically to Python's json module. A `walk` helper for view rule 4.1 number and key validation is being added.\n\nThe `walk_contract_rules_11_12` helper is being implemented to validate contract JSON against rules 11 and 12. It detects invalid numbers, including floating-point values and integers exceeding the safe integer limit, and rejects dictionary keys containing non-printable ASCII characters.\n\nThe author added rule 13 validation to reject contracts whose `establishes` or `does_not_establish` fields are not non-empty lists. Each string in those lists is checked against reserved pattern prefixes, and any match causes the contract to be refused.\n\nThe `does_not_establish` list is lowercased and checked for required keyword groups: runtime, enforce, judge, liability, fault, deviat, and legal. The door function will be a simple pass-or-fail sequence returning False on the first failure.\n\nThe `door` function will return `'ok'`, `'fail'`, or `'carried'`. Parent contracts allow well-formed approvers to pass, while child contracts return `'carried'` when approvers are present. Out-of-scope checks run before `door`, so a child with well-formed approvers will not reach the door.\n\nThe author confirmed that a null carried value passes the door check because the key is allowed. They also noted that carrying out-of-scope contracts triggers an error even when other requirements are invalid. The implementation will be kept under 2000 lines and will use `sys.exit(2)` for errors without printing extra text.\n\nAn empty top-level JSON string will be rejected as invalid_json. The parser will be tested by importing module-level functions directly, since the example contract may lack view headers. A `__main__` guard will be added to support direct execution.\n\nThe author re-examined rule 6's skip condition. An action that is a conditional entry but not authorized or prohibited is correctly kept for requires matching. A conflict between authorized and prohibited is impossible if view rule 4.1 passed.\n\nRule 9 is being added to require a revocation object with an effective_at field. Anchor batch refusals will omit the refusal code and output only the reason. The anchor check will use an if/elif chain to enforce 7.4's first-failure rule. A draft of `check_anchor` validates that the record height and anchor block hash are present and hex.\n\nThe `check_anchor` function now enforces the full anchor validation chain: incomplete data, height below the lower bound, height beyond the observed chain, mismatched block hash, invalid proof, and refused batch leg. It returns one of five codes: accepted, anchor_incomplete, anchor_below_lower_bound, anchor_beyond_observed_chain, orphaned, anchor_proof_invalid, or anchor_batch_leg_refused. The author noted that `anchors_validly` reuses similar checks but omits the lower-bound and incomplete early-return checks.\n\nThe author clarified that a record height below the lower bound is still validly anchored if the view holds that height, but it is not in the observed chain. The `proof_ok` function computes the body digest and Merkle root from the view, then returns True only when `apply_proof` succeeds.\n\nBody digest will be computed from the raw record excluding signatures and anchor. The author will begin with affine Edwards coordinates for Ed25519 verification, deferring extended coordinates if tests prove too slow. The file will be written in chunks, tested, and then returned as a code block.\n\nThe author is reconsidering whether to triage bound records when earlier sections fail. Section 9.2 states that a bound record examined by triage receives no disposition if sections 4, 5, or 6.2 fail. This conflicts with section 2.3, which mandates triage before contract checks and treats problems as independent. The author must resolve this contradiction.\n\nThe author resolved the conflict by prioritizing section 9.2. When sections 4 or 5 fail, bound records are not examined and therefore receive no disposition. This means triage is skipped entirely when rules 4.1, 4.2, or 5.1 fail, so reasons from section 6.0 will not appear for those records.\n\nThe author re-read section 2.3 and concluded that triage still occurs when rules 4.1, 4.2, or 5.1 fail; the phrase \"no record is examined further\" means only later steps are skipped. This means bound records can still receive dispositions even if earlier checks fail.\n\nThe author resolved the earlier conflict by interpreting section 9.2 as the precise rule: bound records are never examined when sections 4, 5, or 6.2 fail, so they receive no disposition. Section 2.3 is treated as loose about order. Records examined in triage but set aside due to size limits still retain their dispositions.\n\nThe author decided to keep dispositions for records examined during triage even when section 6.2 fails, since those records were still examined. They noted that test vectors will not contain thousands of records, so this behavior matches the expected output.\n\nThe author settled on a concrete execution order: SHA-256, door and view checks, binding, then conditional triage. Binding proceeds even when the contract is an object but its events list is empty. Rule 4.3 reasons will not halt processing; malformed bonds still allow triage and walking to continue.\n\nThe author confirmed that malformed bonds still produce dispositions and an underspecified reason, even when deviation codes are empty. They also verified that oversized headers stop examination and receive no disposition, while view-not-strict errors do not trigger a chain-view-rejected reason. Chain tip inclusion in the status is conditional on whether the verdict remains undetermined.\n\nThe author will add a helper to safely convert JInt values to Python integers before JSON serialization. Only known deviation codes from the appendix will be emitted. The Ed25519 group order L is confirmed as 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493.\n\nThe author will hardcode the base point B and compute it at module load, asserting that [L]B equals the identity to catch scalar multiplication bugs early.\n\nThe author will test the program before finalizing it, and may remove the module-load assertion if it proves too brittle. They also verified the Ed25519 curve parameter d and derived the x-coordinate recovery formula for affine point decompression.\n\nThe author is now writing and running the implementation. They are verifying the Ed25519 x-coordinate recovery formula against the RFC 8032 standard, specifically checking the base point case where y = 4/5 and the recovered x must be even.","format":"xai-responses-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoieC1haS9ncm9rLTQuNy0yMDI2MDkxNnx4YWkifQ","format":"xai-responses-v1","id":"rs_b8fec65d-33e0-9e51-bc9f-a00fa5fbe138","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":24278,"completion_tokens":90146,"total_tokens":114424,"cost":0.587704,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.587704,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.046828,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.540876},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":90106,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}